arch/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/not-judged-display/DESIGN.md` |\n915:| D571 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (council rounds 38 to 40): the product page reads the new system's events, theme ranges and unrecorded shapes without inventing a direction (neutral, outcome or range named, no composite) and shows D397's parts not judged; proved by tests, mutants and the fortnight's page in headless Chrome | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/page-contract/RESULT.md` |\n916:| D572 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (council rounds 41 to 43): the page slices of D570 and D571 accepted after round 41's four fixes and the drawer checks; next a $0 check of the API serving a new-system store to the page, before and after a closing | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/page-contract/RESULT.md` |\n917:| D573 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (council round 44): the API serves a new-system store to the page (dates, closings, shapes, the page's cards); the page's one-time read and date clamp observed; next a $0 check of serving while a stored replay writes | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/page-contract/SERVE-RESULT.md` |\n918:| D574 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (council rounds 45 to 50): a freshly opened page shows the served library's latest completed day (the startup read passes the served day; later replacements clamp as before); a live store's first day in progress is an open defect (a race-free guard not built); next, the $0 live-service design | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/page-contract/LIVE-READ-DESIGN.md` |\n919:| D575 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (council rounds 51 and 52): the live-service design paused as recorded input; one consolidated decision page for Alex now; then a $0 feasibility census of Stage B's control line within its fixed population, no paid send implied | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/DECISION-PAGE-2026-11-16.md` |\n920:| D576 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (council rounds 53 to 58): Alex's page stands; Stage B's control line cannot be exercised by re-sending the judge (4 of 8; the blockers set upstream); no delivery check; next, at $0, a census of the unlisted-parts class (line 2) now that the page reads the list, back to the council before any arm | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/stage-b-controls/CENSUS.md` |\n921:| D577 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (council rounds 59 to 65): the unlisted-parts probe of arm A (the topic's wording defined as its description and outcome) sent on 18 stored requests, unchanged and changed ×3 the same day, read blind with planted controls and scored by a rule fixed before the send; a diagnostic, not acceptance | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/DESIGN.md` |\n922:| D578 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (council round 66): arm A's probe negative under both countings (no unlisted parts in changed written answers against 4-5 control topics, but 2 unjustified refusals); the line stops; two bounded accounts of the refusals ($0.02 ceiling) before any further design | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/RESULT.md` |\n923:| D579 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (council round 67): the two refusal accounts apply line 6's decline rules to the outcome's words; the proposed outcome-only exemption refused (D397, D417); a bounded $0 audit of the decline rules and the outcome's extra parts, then a sourced rule or no candidate | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/RESULT.md` |\n924:| D580 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (council rounds 68 and 69): no reviewed repair for the unlisted parts (the decisions give no general rule for the main part of a multi-part topic; an independent reader left 29 of 63 open); arm A stays stopped; next, a $0 check of the Idea planner on new-system answer shapes | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/DECLINE-AUDIT.md` |\n925:| D581 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (council rounds 70 to 72): the Idea planner shows a three-choice event as Yes and Still open; the event-driver design parked as recorded input; no bounded gate-moving candidate on the record; paid work and new lines held until Alex returns or the council names one | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/idea-planner/RESULT.md` |\n948:| D604 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (council round 145): the next production step is a $0 trace of the fresh topics' line-5 failures (each topic's three control answers beside topic, source and settled marks; the mark that changes its status under each counting; family agreement; product visibility), mapped to existing censuses before any candidate; closes this route if no sourced common mechanism | in force | `reflection-4/council-codex-145.md` |\n949:| D605 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (council rounds 146, 147): D604's line-5 route closes with no candidate (fresh misses are reference choices whose rule-following form gives the same answer choices, a stopped class, Alex's boundary, and a reader discrepancy on D414); the gate stays unmet; no paid send without a new reviewable candidate; resume on Alex's rulings or new evidence | in force | `unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/line5/RESULT.md` |\n950:| D606 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (council round 148), reversibly: next product test from a real earlier library (empty starts as diagnostics); Alex's six lines and accounting stay in force; postponement provisionally reads Still open (reversed by D607); D520/D571 running behaviour retained; P1 not taken; next a $0 audit of the postponed-event class (by topic and matter, full rungs, guards) | in force | `reflection-4/council-codex-148.md` |\n951:| D607 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (council round 149): D606's postponement reading reversed provisionally (a formal decision that a dated event will not happen by its due day rules it out; authorities by D420; forecast-versus-decision guard kept); the postponement repair route closes (no missing general rule; Taiwan p3 isolated); next a $0 audit of the shared fiscal-year note's consumers and effects | in force | `unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/postponement/AUDIT-RESULT.md` |\n952:| D608 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (council round 150): the fiscal-note lead closes as cleanup-only (no demonstrated product effect; the clause is not inert because check 5 refuses a pre-year-end day; the reader split's cause not established); a reflection council (fresh Codex, cold read) follows | in force | `unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/fiscal-note/RESULT.md` |\n953:| D609 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (reflection council, cold Codex read): the next production step is a $0 source-to-request trace of the company's own figures on the carried fortnight (D505), by member, with time, duplicate, population and relevance controls and an independent second reader on bearing cases; moves only on a shared, consequential delivery mechanism | in force | `reflection-5/codex-cold.md`, `reflection-5/codex-challenge-1.md` |\n954:| D610 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (reflection council): the company-figure delivery route closes narrowly (5 bearing undelivered statements on 3 questions, two mechanisms, none predicted to move an answer by a blind second reader with working controls; 49 groups homeless by D382/D384); no v12 effect test; next a $0 feasibility review of a same-news product comparison from established libraries | in force | `delivery-trace/RESULT.md` |\n955:| D611 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439: the same-news comparison from established libraries closes (D517's power arithmetic unchanged by a starting library; the old library ends 08-28; no exposure-free week remains from 09-21 to 10-26); nothing run | in force | `comparison-feasibility/RESULT.md` |\n956:| D612 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (council round 151): paid work held on the currently supported candidates (every bounded route closed with evidence; A2 cannot be decided on stored topics); the running system stays the default; reopen on new consequential delivery cases, decidable comparison material, Alex's marks or decisions, or a new concrete reversible candidate | in force | `reflection-4/council-codex-151.md` |\n957:| D613 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (council round 152): D611's exposure claim corrected (07-20..08-24 unread by any new-system Step 1 run); D612's hold stands; official gate 1 already meets lines 2 and 5 on the 44 and misses zero-fault lines 1, 3, 4 by 6 sends of 131 of different meanings; no code check or send on them | in force | `reflection-4/council-codex-152.md` |\n958:| D614 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (council round 153): the refusal census (174 over 30 topics; 13 unjustified on 6, all 'none main') maps to the closed main-part class; D612 stands; next a $0 design review of splitting (separate topics vs two judged questions under one topic) against D397, D436, D38 with the guards; no version opened, D397 unchanged | in force | `refusal-census/RESULT.md` |\n959:| D615 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (council round 154): the period split (D38's one view a year; the step names a typed 'one quantity for two periods' case, code splits by the years named) is developed at $0 as a lead on D397's open defect; no gate movement claimed; probe only after a reviewed design | in force | `splitting/DESIGN-REVIEW.md` |\n960:| D616 | 2026-11-16 | Under D439 (council rounds 155 to 158): the period split's design v4 accepted for a $0 scratch build beside the frozen ladder step; the narrow D397 departure (one judged question per open year for one quantity over two open years) recorded as a reversible proposal, not in force; Stage B readers refuse split stores; nothing sent | in force | `splitting/DESIGN.md` |\n987:| D643 | 2026-11-16 | Gate-1 launch prepared as an inert draft for Alex (`reflection-6/gate1-candidate/`): Stage A on the 44 × 3 with astra-frozen or astra-a2-v2, byte-checked, preflighted (148 stored attempts, 0 differ), refuses without authorization, NOT MEASURED on a stop, ~$4.90–6.60, cap ≥ ~$9 (beyond D439); mutants not run (permission refused) | in force | `reflection-6/gate1-candidate/README.md`, `reflection-6/codex-challenge-36.md`–`38.md` |\n988:| D644 | 2026-11-16 | Council round 39: a fifth Stage A within the remaining budget (sol + A2 + v2) refused: D426 is Alex's specific decision not reversed by D439's general delegation, and the pair is unmeasured; no in-authority, in-budget step moves the production decision; the record waits on Alex | in force | `reflection-6/codex-challenge-39.md` |\n999:| D655 | 2026-11-17 | Council consensus (D439, D654): send the paired test of the narrow passage v5.5 on astra (3 deciding targets, 2 reported, 5 controls, a share control; 52 sends, shared cap $3.60); verdict rules fixed before the send | in force | D655 record |\n1021:| D677 | 2026-11-19 | The release comparison's goal loop closed: the old system stays, no paid work continues; reversed only if Alex reverses D505 or funds a full run, with a written path to passing first (council consensus, D439/D544) | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-19-comparison-review/council-c-codex.md` |\n1028:| D684 | 2026-11-20 | Under D683: Alex approves the presentation-only test and hands the phase-1 iteration to Claude under the council loop (D439 limits); fresh pool of 116 answers as of 09-06 that the design never read | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/DESIGN-J2.md` |\n1033:| D689 | 2026-11-20 | Alex chooses option 1: poc-v118 stays production, the hybrid's release line is paused, the release bar unchanged; council N (fresh Codex thread, consensus) closes the judge line and the c8 reading line for now under D543, D198 unchanged, nothing sent; reopening: judge on eight eligible failures on fresh questions plus matched controls and a source-checked summary (D198 option 2), then Alex's choice and funding; c8 on a traced decisive fact it delivers that old missed; D439 accounting error recorded ($39.65–$44.42 under two readings; $0 available without Alex) | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/council-n-codex.md` |\n1034:| D690 | 2026-11-20 | Alex chooses A: poc-v118 is the product and the research line rests until his new direction; B (other companies' own figures, the judge's carried conclusion) and C (new paid test questions) not taken; D689's reopening conditions stand; D681–D690 records committed locally, not pushed | in force | `docs/DECISIONS-log.md` |\n1035:| D691 | 2026-11-20 | Alex chooses A after council Q (library useful for sources, not its number answers: linked questions contradict): no daily wrapper yet; link related questions first, a $0 check then a few-cents probe; the dependency stretch D533–D542 stays a provisional candidate | in force | `docs/DECISIONS-log.md` |\n1036:| D692 | 2026-11-20 | Under Alex's A: the dependency probe passes (linked 2 of 3 reconcile, unchanged 0 of 3; $0.0176); the stored extreme did not recur; the dependency design review reopens at $0, then Alex; the self-made reference traced apart | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-probe/RESULT.md` |\n1256:## D51 · 2026-10-21 · Claude's open items settled: one re-judging rule, dependencies chosen in the matching call, no consensus\n3084:## D265 · 2026-11-02 · Extraction v72 / poc-v92 is the version: the default configuration, the base every later change builds on, the control of every later measurement; D207's narrowest rule is enforced in code\n3685:## D354 · 2026-11-03 · Steps 3 and 4 (opening and matching questions, judging them) are frozen as `poc-v108` runs them, after three draws on the frozen extraction\n3960:## D368 · 2026-11-05 · Alex's answers to the seven items left open by the consolidation\n4738:## D427 · 2026-11-11 · Clean Step 2 is closed and frozen; it goes to production only with the whole clean path\n4871:## D439 · 2026-11-13 · While Alex is not active, the council decides by consensus and the loop iterates to the PRD's production goals\n6375:## D533 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: this stretch of the lean investigation is closed and consolidated; the default and both systems stay; next a separate $0 assessment of D368's dependency channel on a stored, correctly directed case (council consensus, reflection round 24)\n6394:## D534 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the dependency-channel assessment is corrected (its lines came from later conclusions); a $0 chronology and ordering proof comes before any measurement design (council consensus, reflection round 25)\n6411:## D535 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the one-chain ordering proof of D368's direction is accepted with its limits; a source-backed case and a verified pre-judgement state of 2026-07-14 come before any measurement design (council consensus, reflection round 26)\n6432:## D536 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: no measurement of the reversed reading direction is sent now; next, at $0, size where dependency selection could act in the stored unseeded fresh week (council consensus, reflection round 27)\n6457:## D537 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the dependency-selection census has one clear case and negative controls; before any send, the edge's effect on filing and judging is proved on stored data (council consensus, reflection round 28)\n6479:## D538 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: one dependency edge shows the intended conclusion to the judge and removes seven filing links; the dependency slice's design is opened at $0, beginning with the separation of the edge's two uses (council consensus, reflection round 29)\n6504:## D539 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the separation of a dependency edge's two uses is kept as a candidate, not yet the builder's contract; its design continues at $0 (council consensus, reflection round 30)\n6525:## D540 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the separation stays a provisional research candidate; the filing and duplicate-label contracts are finished at $0 before any selection design or send (council consensus, reflection round 31)\n6544:## D541 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the seed edge's label stops everything that touches a seed edge; the filing finding is bounded; the unseeded failure-and-reader contract is finished at $0 before the separation is called sound (council consensus, reflection round 32)\n6566:## D542 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the dependency stretch is closed with the separation recorded as a provisional candidate; the status is consolidated; nothing is sent (council consensus, reflection round 33)\n6589:## D543 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the cold reflection on the lean path: D518 stands with its reasons, the choice of system is not made in Alex's place, and no investigation is opened (reflection council consensus)\n10360:## D690 · 2026-11-20 · Alex chooses A: poc-v118 is the product, the research line rests until his new direction; the D681–D689 records are committed locally\n10375:## D691 · 2026-11-20 · Alex chooses A after council Q: no daily wrapper yet; link related questions first, a $0 check then a few cents\n10398:## D692 · 2026-11-20 · Under Alex's A (D691): the dependency probe passes its registered rule; the dependency design review reopens at $0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/SEPARATION-DESIGN.md | sed -n '1,264p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/SEPARATION-DESIGN.md | sed -n '1,264p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Design for the council's review: the two uses of a dependency edge, read apart (D538, under D439), 2026-11-15, $0\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: **closed as a provisional research candidate, not sound for build** (council rounds 30 to 33, D542), on paper, with two\n     4\tproofs on stored data; not a finished product rule. If the slice is reopened, its review must settle section 7 before any build. Nothing is built, nothing was sent, no\n     5\tpackage, configuration or frozen file changed. It does not decide the stored representation, does not change D368's direction,\n     6\tand does not design the selection call itself (who chooses, what it is shown): those are listed in section 7. Revised after\n     7\tround 30: the reading of D265 is stated as a proposed interpretation with its cost (2.1); the duplicate and circle rule is\n     8\texplicit (2.2); rows written before a kind was stored have one versioned reading (2.3); the one-home requirement is stated as\n     9\tunresolved for questions opened from articles (2.4); the reader table is completed (4); a second proof exercises the production\n    10\twriters and a whole week (9).\n    11\t\n    12\t## 1. The defect, in one picture\n    13\t\n    14\tOne stored row, `dependencies(view_id, depends_on, direction, ...)`, has two readers:\n    15\t\n    16\t| Reader | Where | What it takes the row to mean |\n    17\t|---|---|---|\n    18\t| judging | `store.dependencies_of`, `store.dependants_of`, `store.would_cycle` (the request's block, the order of a round, the citations allowed, what a changed conclusion queues, the cycle check) | \"this question reads that question's conclusion\" |\n    19\t| filing | `store.direct_parents` → `extraction.narrowest_links` (D265) | \"this question is the narrower home: a statement linked to both keeps this one only\" |\n    20\t\n    21\tand two kinds of writer mean different things by it:\n    22\t\n    23\t| Writer | What the row states | Judging as coded | Filing as coded |\n    24\t|---|---|---|---|\n    25\t| a seed file (`seeding.seed_views`; D208: \"each driver declaring the root it feeds\") | the driver **supports** the root | wrong way: the driver reads the root (D368 item 4; traced 2026-11-03, `../../2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/core-problem.md`) | right: the driver is the narrower home |\n    26\t| the matching call for a new question (D51, PRD 06; not asked on the default path today) | the new question **reads** the other's conclusion | right | not stated by the row, yet applied: the reader is treated as the narrower home (`EDGE-PROOF-RESULT.md`: seven statements lose their total-revenue link) |\n    27\t\n    28\t## 2. The rule\n    29\t\n    30\tEach edge carries its kind, set by its writer and by nothing else: `supports` or `reads`. Each reader reads one derived relation.\n    31\t\n    32\t- **Reading relation** (judging): for a `reads` edge (B, A), B reads A's conclusion. For a `supports` edge (D, R), R reads D's.\n    33\t- **Narrower relation** (filing): for a `supports` edge (D, R), D is the narrower home than R. A `reads` edge states none.\n    34\t\n    35\tSources: D368 item 4 (\"For filing, the supporting (driver) question is the narrower one ... For judging, the supported (root)\n    36\tquestion reads the supporting questions' conclusions ... The fix separates the two uses of the edge\"), which speaks of seed\n    37\tedges; PRD 06 (\"B depending on A means B reads A's conclusion\"; \"A statement about A is A's evidence ... B learns it through A's\n    38\tconclusion\").\n    39\t\n    40\t### 2.1 What the rule changes in a decision's reading, and what it costs\n    41\t\n    42\tD265 says: \"a statement linked to a question and to a question that question depends on (the ledger's `dependencies`, the seeds'\n    43\troot-and-driver pairs, transitively) keeps the narrower one only\". The rule keeps this for `supports` edges and **does not apply\n    44\tit to `reads` edges**. That is a proposed interpretation, not something D368 settles: D368 separates the two uses for seed edges\n    45\tand does not say that a dependency chosen by the matching call has no filing meaning. The council may take the interpretation\n    46\tunder D439; it is recorded as one.\n    47\t\n    48\tIts cost, measured on the one real case: with the `reads` edge \"data-center revenue reads total revenue\", the seven statements the\n    49\treading step linked to both questions keep both homes, as they do today without any edge. Four of them belong under total revenue\n    50\t(the company-wide outlook; three sentences holding its own reported figure); three are about the segment's competition and D207's\n    51\tnarrowest home would be data-center revenue alone. So the interpretation preserves the four and leaves the three filed twice.\n    52\tThe literal reading of D265 does the opposite: it moves the three rightly and strips the four wrongly, one of them a statement\n    53\ta total-revenue judgement cites.\n    54\t\n    55\t### 2.2 Duplicates and circles, on the reading relation\n    56\t\n    57\tAn edge is proposed with its kind; the writer looks at the reading pair it would add (who would read whom).\n    58\t\n    59\t| The reading relation already holds | The proposed edge adds | Outcome |\n    60\t|---|---|---|\n    61\t| B reads A (from `reads` (B, A)) | B reads A again, the same direction label | a duplicate: not written |\n    62\t| B reads A (from `reads` (B, A)) | B reads A again, **another direction label** | **a conflict, refused and recorded, never dropped as a duplicate** (PRD 06: \"A dependency has one direction\"; within one answer the existing check already refuses \"same-key dependency direction conflicts\") |\n    63\t| B reads A (from `reads` (B, A)) | A reads B (`reads` (A, B)) | refused as a circle |\n    64\t| R reads D (from `supports` (D, R)) | D reads R (`reads` (D, R)) | refused as a circle |\n    65\t| R reads D (from `supports` (D, R)) | R reads D again (`reads` (R, D), any label) | **not resolvable here: returned to the council.** The seed edge's label is `last_end`, written as a constant by the seeding module; no decision, design page, seed file or prompt defines it, so \"the same direction\" and \"a conflicting one\" cannot be told apart without inventing its meaning. The proof writes nothing and reports \"label undefined\" |\n    66\t| a longer path from the read question back to the reader, through edges of either kind | any edge that closes it | refused as a circle (PRD 06: \"Before adding an edge ... code checks for cycles\") |\n    67\t\n    68\tThe production writer has the circle check and a duplicate check within one write; the conflict across articles and the handling\n    69\tof a pair already given by the other kind would be the builder's.\n    70\t\n    71\t### 2.3 Rows written before a kind was stored\n    72\t\n    73\tA writer stores the kind when it writes the edge, and a reader never infers it from equal values. For rows already in stored\n    74\tledgers there is one versioned reading, by marks (`edge_inventory.py`, `edge-inventory.json`). The package's history shows two\n    75\twriters of the table (the seeding module, D253; the writer step from a matching answer). What was counted, and no more: the 158\n    76\tSQLite files under `poc/data` whose name contains \"views\" (snapshot folders included); 99 hold rows. **1,138** rows carry the\n    77\tseeding module's marks (sequence 0, direction `last_end`, on a question created from a seed file) and are read as `supports`;\n    78\t**395** sit on a question opened from an article, at the very article that opened it, with a direction label of the matching\n    79\tvocabulary (its English form or the earlier Chinese one), and are read as `reads`, the meaning judging has always given them;\n    80\tnone waits for a year; **none** is anything else. The count classes rows by their marks; it does not identify a writer beyond\n    81\tthem and says nothing of a ledger kept elsewhere. A build refuses a row with neither set of marks and no stored kind (it raises\n    82\tand names the row; exercised), and rewrites no stored ledger.\n    83\t\n    84\t### 2.4 The one-home requirement: what is represented, and what is left unresolved\n    85\t\n    86\tA hierarchy between two questions is represented by a `supports` edge and by nothing else; only the seeding module writes one.\n    87\tFor questions opened from articles no decided writer states a hierarchy, with or without this design, so a statement the reading\n    88\tstep links to two such questions keeps both homes. In the fresh week 60 of 385 stored records have more than one home; that\n    89\tsizes such records and is **not** a count of breaches: D360 itself stores a sentence that gives a figure for each of two\n    90\tquestions as one record linked to both (corrected after the council's round 31).\n    91\t\n    92\t### 2.5 The filing contract, case by case (`filing_contract.py`, `filing-contract.json`)\n    93\t\n    94\tEach case: what the reading step linked (from the stored raw answer), what the code's rule leaves with and without a stated\n    95\thierarchy between the two questions, and the homes the existing decisions call for. The expected homes are written from the\n    96\tdecisions by hand; no code decides them.\n    97\t\n    98\t| Case | The reading step linked | No stated hierarchy (today for article-opened questions; a `reads` edge) | A stated hierarchy (a `supports` edge; D265 read literally on a `reads` edge) | Expected, and by which words |\n    99\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n   100\t| the company-wide outlook excluding China | both | both: no | data-center only: no | total revenue only. D207: \"company-wide figure questions take only their own figures, guidance and management statements\" |\n   101\t| the quarter's total and segment revenue in one sentence (three outlets) | both, as one item | both: yes | data-center only: no | both. D360: \"a statement giving a separate figure for each of two questions is written as two evidence items, one under each\" |\n   102\t| a competitor's segment revenue; a customer buying capacity elsewhere; the same customer designing its own chips | both | both: no | data-center only: yes | data-center only. D207, and the frozen link rule: \"compare the specific questions before a company-wide revenue or margin question and take that one only\" |\n   103\t| seeded draw: one matter of the driver (the spot price of GPU time) | both | both: no | data-center only: yes | data-center only (D207, D259) |\n   104\t| seeded draw: the quarter's total and segment revenue in one sentence | both, as one item | both: yes | data-center only: no (the cost PRD 10 §3 records) | both (D360) |\n   105\t| a two-figure sentence written as two items, one link each (D360's own form; synthetic) | one item under each | both: yes | both: yes | both (D360) |\n   106\t| the unrelated pair (Hugging Face commitments; financing commitments) | no statement is under both | unchanged | unchanged | n/a |\n   107\t\n   108\tFive of the ten cases come out as expected under each state, and a different five. **No reading of an edge yields the expected\n   109\thomes**, because in every real case the reading step wrote one item linked to both questions, and telling the whole's own\n   110\tstatement from a driver's is a judgement about what the statement is, not an identity code can hold (AGENTS §2). Who owns what:\n   111\t\n   112\t| Decision | Kind | Owner |\n   113\t|---|---|---|\n   114\t| which question a statement bears on, and which it measures most directly | judgement | the reading step, under the frozen link rule |\n   115\t| one item or two for a sentence that holds a figure for each of two questions | judgement | the reading step (D360; its sentence is not in the frozen reading prompt, whose version carrying it failed its test, D361) |\n   116\t| whether two questions are a driver and its root | identity | the writer of a `supports` edge (the seed file's author today) |\n   117\t| of two linked questions with a stated hierarchy, keep the narrower | identity, given the hierarchy | code (D265), with the recorded cost when the reading step links the root's own figure to both |\n   118\t| whether a question reads another's conclusion | judgement | the matching call for a new question (D51); code checks circles, duplicates and conflicts only |\n   119\t\n   120\tSo the contract this design can state for filing is narrow: **a `reads` edge moves no statement; a `supports` edge keeps D265's\n   121\trule and its recorded cost; the one home for the cases above is the reading step's to write.** The design adds no rule to make\n   122\tcode produce those homes, and none is proposed here.\n   123\t\n   124\t### 2.6 The unseeded path: what can happen to a dependency chosen at creation, and who already handles it (round 32)\n   125\t\n   126\tToday's default holds no seed edge (D370), so only `reads` edges written when a question is created (D51) are in play. For each\n   127\tevent: whether it can occur at creation, what the decisions say, what the code does today, and what exercised it under the\n   128\tseparated reading (`seam_plugin.py` runs the package's own 287 tests with the seam installed; `old_rows_readers.py`).\n   129\t\n   130\t| Event | At creation? | Decision | Code today | Under the candidate | Exercised |\n   131\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n   132\t| an edge chosen for a new question | yes | D51, PRD 06 | `matching.check`, then `writing.write` in the article's commit | unchanged; the writer stores `reads` | the whole week (section 9, W2) |\n   133\t| the answer names a question not shown, itself, or a candidate that is not opened | yes | PRD 06: \"Choose only among ...\" | `matching.check` refuses; the article is retried once (one attempt on the Jev engine) and then fails, resumable | unchanged | the suite under the seam |\n   134\t| a circle among the candidates of one answer; conflicting directions between same-key candidates | yes | PRD 06: \"Refuse a cycle and ask the model to correct it; persistent cycles follow normal failure handling\"; \"A dependency has one direction\" | `matching.check` and `identity.batch_plan` refuse; the refusal is in the match record | unchanged | the suite under the seam (`test_coalesced_dependency_is_refused_before_matching_retry_ends`) |\n   135\t| a circle against edges already stored | **yes, in one way** (corrected after round 33): nothing reads a question that is being created, except an existing question that was waiting for it. If the new question names that waiting question as its dependency, binding the waiting edge would close a circle; `resolve_waiting` checks and leaves the edge unbound, without a record | PRD 06, as above | `writing.write` drops the edge with a note in the article's record. **This differs from PRD 06 (refuse and ask for a correction)**; the difference predates this design and can only matter for a later addition | the cycle check walks the reading relation | proofs 1 and 2 (the check itself) |\n   136\t| a second edge for the same pair at a later article (a duplicate, or another direction) | **no**: only the later addition (D191 point 2, unbuilt) can produce it | \"A dependency has one direction\"; **what happens to a later conflicting proposal is not written in any decision** | no code path | 2.2 states the outcomes; the conflict's handling is recorded for the council, not decided here | the seam's outcomes (section 9, X) |\n   137\t| the read question closes, gains a successor, or its awaited year is created | yes | PRD 06's tree (read the closing conclusion; read the successor and judge once; wait and bind) | the request marks \"closed\" or \"historical only\"; resolution rows; `resolve_waiting` with the cycle check | unchanged: `reads` rows pass through the same functions | the suite under the seam (the awaited year; resolutions in the bundle and the research context). No test covers \"historical only\", and no code in the package writes that resolution (the request can print the mark; nothing sets it): PRD 06's branch for a question closed without a successor has a reader and no writer |\n   138\t| the question named is a theme | yes | PRD 06's own example reads an event question; D195 also shows every theme to every company question | for a company question both are printed: the conclusion appears twice; a theme is not shown the theme block | unchanged (it predates this design) | not exercised. The census holds three theme-to-theme pairs both readers grant (next year's market spending reading this year's, strongly; the two Chinese-permission questions and the U.S. authorization, weakly, both ways) and no company question reading a theme that both grant |\n   139\t| a crash after the article's commit; a round that dies midway | yes | D63 | the edge is in the commit or absent; a resumed round rebuilds its queue through the dependants | the queue reads the reading relation | the suite under the seam with one fixture's row restated as a chosen dependency: the resume test passes |\n   140\t| a row written before a kind was stored, read by the pages, the search records and the idea route | n/a | PRD 06 | `service.dependencies` and `service.dependants` read the stored row | for a `reads` row the service shows exactly what the candidate reads | all 399 questions with an edge in the 28 ledgers that hold such rows |\n   141\t\n   142\tThe package's suite under the seam: 285 of 287 pass as they are. The two that do not both write a row by hand with the seed\n   143\tlabel at an article's sequence, which neither writer produces: the resume test (it passes once that row is restated as a chosen\n   144\tdependency: 286) and `test_a_statement_filed_under_a_driver_and_its_root_keeps_the_driver_only`, which holds D265 in its literal\n   145\treading over such rows and would state `supports` for them under the candidate.\n   146\t\n   147\tSo on the unseeded path the events that can occur at creation are handled by code the candidate does not touch, and most were\n   148\texercised; **not exercised**: a theme named by a company question, a question closed without a successor, and the circle through\n   149\tan awaited question. The suite under the seam is evidence of compatibility, not a proof of every event; the old rows were read\n   150\tthrough the service at one far date, not at every historical day. What the candidate leaves undetermined: the later addition\n   151\t(its conflict and its cycle handling), and everything that touches a seed edge (the label, 2.2; the seeded pages, where the\n   152\tservice shows the stored direction or, for most seed rows, nothing).\n   153\t\n   154\t## 3. Table 1: state of a pair by event\n   155\t\n   156\t\"Seq\" is dispatch's sequence number of the article (or 0 for a seed file); no cell reads the clock.\n   157\t\n   158\t| Event | No edge | `reads` edge (B, A) | `supports` edge (D, R) |\n   159\t|---|---|---|---|\n   160\t| a question B is created and the matching call names A (D51) | cycle check on the reading relation, then the edge is written with kind `reads` at the article's seq, in the same commit as B (`writing.write` and the \"written\" record are one commit); B's first judgement that evening reads A's conclusion | the writer's own duplicate check writes nothing twice (a resumed article) | a new B has no edge yet: not reachable |\n   161\t| a later article names a dependency for an existing question (D191 point 2; decided, unbuilt) | cycle check, then a `reads` edge at that article's seq; the question is judged once in that day's round (its inputs changed, PRD 06) | nothing written | the same pair the other way round: a circle, refused (next row) |\n   162\t| the proposed edge would close a circle in the reading relation | refused: nothing written, a note in the article's record (\"dropped: cycle\", as `writing.py` does today); the question opens without it | as left | as left |\n   163\t| the read question's conclusion changes (its judgement says so) | nothing | B is queued in the same round, after A | D changes: R is queued after D. R changes: D is **not** queued (today it is) |\n   164\t| the read question closes | nothing | with a successor: a resolution row to the successor, B judged once; without: B reads the closing conclusion marked \"historical only\" (PRD 06, as today) | R reads D's closing conclusion as \"historical only\"; seed edges have no successor rule. Unexercised: no stored draw closes a question |\n   165\t| the awaited year's question is created (a waiting `reads` edge) | nothing | bound by `resolve_waiting` after the cycle check; B judged once (as today) | a seed edge carries no awaited year: not reachable |\n   166\t| the reading step links one statement to both questions | both links stay (today) | both links stay: the edge states no hierarchy | D's link stays, R's is removed (D265), from the first day whose day-start list holds the edge (its seq at or before the list's) |\n   167\t| a crash between writing the question and the day's judging; a resume | nothing | the edge is in the article's commit or not at all; a resumed round rebuilds its queue from the committed judgements through the same reading relation (`queued_by_ledger`) | the same |\n   168\t| a later run on a later day on the same store | nothing | the kind is stored with the edge: read the same under any configuration | the same |\n   169\t| a row written before a kind was stored (2.3) | nothing | a row at an article's seq, on a question opened from an article, with a matching direction label: `reads`, as judging has always read it | a row with the seeding module's marks: `supports`. A row with neither writer's marks: refused |\n   170\t| a second edge between the same two questions (2.2) | n/a | the same reading pair again: not written; the opposite pair: a circle, refused | `reads` (R, D), the pair the edge already gives: not written; `reads` (D, R): a circle, refused |\n   171\t| the reading question closes | nothing | a closed question is not judged (as today); the edge stays as history | the same |\n   172\t| work in flight when a run ends, a second writer, two objects at once | nothing | only the writer step writes edges, one article at a time in sequence order (`dispatch`); the parallel day prepares requests only | the same |\n   173\t\n   174\t## 4. Table 2: each value by its readers\n   175\t\n   176\tReaders found by searching the package for the table and the three store functions.\n   177\t\n   178\t| Reader | Today | Under the rule |\n   179\t|---|---|---|\n   180\t| `judging.topological`, `upstream` (order of a round) | the stored row: the row's question after the question it names | the reading relation |\n   181\t| `judging.build_prompt` (the block \"Current conclusions of the theses it depends on\") | the stored row | the reading relation: for a `supports` edge the root is shown its drivers, a driver is not shown its root |\n   182\t| `judging.check_answer` (`cites_views` may name only what was shown) | the stored row | the reading relation, so the closed set equals what the request showed |\n   183\t| `judging.queued_by_change`, `queued_by_ledger` | the stored row reversed | the reading relation reversed |\n   184\t| `store.would_cycle` (`writing.write`, `resolve_waiting`) | walks the stored rows | walks the reading relation, across both kinds |\n   185\t| `matching.check` and `identity.batch_plan` (dependencies among the candidates of one answer: named only among what was shown, not on itself, no circle, no conflicting direction between same-key candidates) | the candidates' proposed rows | unchanged in what they check: a candidate's chosen dependencies are `reads` edges; the circle among candidates is on the reading relation |\n   186\t| `extraction.shown_views` → `file_reading` → `narrowest_links` | every row is a hierarchy | the narrower relation: `supports` edges only |\n   187\t| `writing.resolve_waiting` (awaited years, successors) | rows with waiting fields | unchanged: only `reads` edges carry them |\n   188\t| `store.views_for_prompt` (a list \"with who depends on whom\") | no caller in the package or the scripts | must not be revived without reading the kind |\n   189\t| `service.dependencies`, `service.dependants`, `bundle`, `search` (the result records), `research_context`, and the idea route (`idea.py` walks \"upstream\" and \"downstream\" through the service) | read the stored rows as \"depends on\" and \"depended on by\" | they expose the meaning, not a label: each must read the reading relation (and show the kind), or the product shows a `supports` edge the wrong way round and the idea route walks it backwards. **Part of any build, not a later step** |\n   190\t| writers: `seeding.seed_views` (retired from the default by D370; each stored seeded draw holds 17 such rows); `writing.write` from the matching answer (`jev_matching` returns `'dependencies': []` today) | write the row without a kind | each writes its kind: the seeding module `supports`, the matching writer `reads`. Kind is never inferred from equal values (AGENTS §3, Identity) |\n   191\t\n   192\tThe stored unseeded default runs hold no edge at all (the fresh week: 0 rows), so under the rule both relations are empty there\n   193\tand every request and every filing of today's default is unchanged.\n   194\t\n   195\t## 5. Table 3: the new reading against the checks the old one passes\n   196\t\n   197\t| Check | Held by | Under the rule |\n   198\t|---|---|---|\n   199\t| today's default: every reading, matching and judging request | the frozen-piece tests; `scripts/preflight.py` | unchanged: no edge exists (section 4) |\n   200\t| a statement under a driver and its root keeps the driver (`test_a_statement_filed_under_a_driver_and_its_root_keeps_the_driver_only`) | seed-style rows | unchanged for `supports` edges. Its fixture inserts rows by hand: it would state their kind |\n   201\t| the stored seeded draw's filing | the stored handovers | reproduced: 243 of 243 (section 8, B) |\n   202\t| a cycle is refused | `store.would_cycle` | refused across both kinds (section 8, C) |\n   203\t| `cites_views` only names what was shown | `judging.check_answer`, `test_the_request_names_the_closed_set_for_cites_views_when_no_dependency_is_listed` | holds: the same relation feeds the block and the check (section 8, A and B) |\n   204\t| dependencies are judged first; a dependant reads this round's conclusion | `round_of_judgements` | holds on the reading relation (section 8, A: total revenue before data-center revenue; B: 11 and 13 pairs) |\n   205\t| a waiting dependency binds when its year's question is created (`test_waiting_dependency_resolves_across_year_spellings`) | `resolve_waiting` | unchanged (`reads` edges only) |\n   206\t| a question with no evidence in force is not judged, whatever it reads (D59) | `round_of_judgements` (`eligible`) | unchanged |\n   207\t| every theme reaches every company question without an edge (D191, D195) | the theme block | unchanged |\n   208\t| the store only appends | the store's triggers | a kind is written with the edge, never updated |\n   209\t| the judgements of a seeded store | stored requests | **change, by D368 item 4's intent** (a root gains the block, a driver loses it): \"it changes judgements, so it is measured before it enters the default\". Not built or measured here (D536) |\n   210\t\n   211\t## 6. Table 4: prior decisions\n   212\t\n   213\t| Decision | Its words | Here |\n   214\t|---|---|---|\n   215\t| D51 (Alex, 2026-10-21) | \"dependencies chosen in the matching call\"; a question \"reads its own evidence and the conclusions (state and reasons) of the views it depends on\" | a chosen dependency is a `reads` edge |\n   216\t| PRD 06, Dependencies | \"B depending on A means B reads A's conclusion\"; \"A statement about A is A's evidence\"; \"Choose only among searched theses, extraction block-5 theses and candidates opened from the same item\"; cycles refused | the reading relation; no hierarchy from a `reads` edge |\n   217\t| D191 (Alex, 2026-10-26) | themes: \"这个 1 to all mapping 比较 make sense\"; \"A dependency may be added to an existing thesis by a later item (可以)\"; \"The `feeds` field of the design is withdrawn for themes\" | themes need no edge; the later addition is a row of table 1, unbuilt |\n   218\t| D207 (Alex, 2026-10-28) | \"one home, the narrowest question; company-wide figure questions take only their own figures, guidance and management statements\" | the narrower relation serves it where a hierarchy is stated; it is not manufactured from a `reads` edge |\n   219\t| D208 (Alex, 2026-10-28, design) | \"each driver declaring the root it feeds through `dependencies`\"; \"Root judgements read driver and theme conclusions, not their evidence\" | a `supports` edge, in both its uses |\n   220\t| D259 (Alex, 2026-11-02) | \"先放款take 吧，辅助反正就是个中间地带我们给点leeway\"; the sink \"bounded by the narrowest-question rule and the driver questions\" | unchanged |\n   221\t| D260 | the prompt's \"a component cannot exceed its whole\" | a part reading its whole stays expressible as a `reads` edge |\n   222\t| D265 (2026-11-02) | \"a statement linked to a question and to a question that question depends on (the ledger's `dependencies`, the seeds' root-and-driver pairs, transitively) keeps the narrower one only\" | kept for `supports` edges. For a `reads` edge the words \"depends on\" no longer imply \"narrower\": the one place this design reads a decision more narrowly than its text; it rests on D368 item 4 and PRD 06, and is put to the council as such |\n   223\t| D360 (Alex: \"go\") | \"a statement giving a separate figure for each of two questions is written as two evidence items, one under each\" | not in the frozen reading prompt (its version failed its test, D361). Under a `supports` edge the root still loses a two-figure sentence (PRD 10 §3: \"0–2 of the facts it needs\" a draw): unchanged here, listed in section 7 |\n   224\t| D368 (2026-11-05) | item 2, Alex: \"做\" (the matching step chooses a new question's dependencies; a later item may add one); item 4, settled by Claude on his delegation (\"好你来定\"): the two sentences quoted in section 2 | item 4's direction is kept as recorded, not measured (D536); this design is its \"fix separates the two uses\", extended to chosen edges (D538) |\n   225\t| D354, D370, D427 | judging, matching and the reading prompt are frozen as they run; preset questions are retired from the default | nothing here changes a frozen piece; a build is a new version the council or Alex opens |\n   226\t| The independent review of 2026-11-03 (`review-core-codex.md`, point 6) | six contracts a fix must settle: filing's use, cycles across kinds, article-opened questions' edges keeping their meaning, the direction label, citations, the 600-character cut | the first, second, third and fifth are answered by the rule and section 8; the fourth and sixth are open (section 7) |\n   227\t\n   228\t## 7. What this design leaves open (for the council; nothing here is assumed)\n   229\t\n   230\t1. **The representation**: a kind on the row, or two tables. The proof derives the kind from the writer's marks for stored\n   231\t   edges; a build would store it where the edge is written.\n   232\t2. **The direction label of a `supports` edge when the root reads the driver: a blocker for any seeded build.** Seed rows carry\n   233\t   `last_end`, outside the matching vocabulary (`first_is_bullish`, `last_is_bullish`, `same`, `opposite`); the request prints\n   234\t   the label as stored, and what it tells the new reader has not been established.\n   235\t3. **What the line carries**: the conclusion's first 600 characters and its band, not the implied outcome; a root might get a\n   236\t   band without the calculation behind it.\n   237\t4. **A theme named by a `reads` edge** would be shown twice (the theme block and the line). The census found no such pair\n   238\t   that both readers grant.\n   239\t5. **The strongest relations of the fresh week are not reachable at creation** (the Rubin shipping questions against the\n   240\t   revenue questions opened before them). Two decided or possible routes, neither built: the later addition (D191 point 2), or\n   241\t   letting a new question declare that it supports an existing one, which is D208's sentence for seeds and beyond D51's words\n   242\t   for article-opened questions. A choice, not assumed here.\n   243\t6. **The known cost of the narrowest rule under a `supports` edge** (the root's own figure in a sentence that also holds the\n   244\t   driver's) is unchanged by this design.\n   245\t7. **The selection itself**: which call is asked, what it is shown (the matching engine already stores a closed relation for\n   246\t   every compared pair, \"part of\" among them), its prompt audit and its measurement. A separate design.\n   247\t8. **D368 item 4's direction** stays recorded and unmeasured; a measurement needs a seeded configuration the default no longer\n   248\t   runs (D536).\n   249\t9. **Cells of table 1 no proof exercises**, which a build-ready contract must fill: a `supports` edge whose driver closes, with or\n   250\t   without a successor (a stored kind affects resolution); the later addition (D191 point 2); a stored ledger with `reads` rows\n   251\t   read through today's pages (the 395 rows are read here only by their marks).\n   252\t10. **The one home for the cases of 2.5**: the reading step's, the filing phase's subject (PRD 10); no rule for code is proposed.\n   253\t11. **Where decisions and code part, or decisions are silent** (2.6): a cycling edge is dropped with a note where PRD 06 says\n   254\t    refuse and ask for a correction; an awaited edge that would close a circle is left unbound without a record; \"historical\n   255\t    only\" has a reader and no writer; no decision says what happens to a later proposal with another direction.\n   256\t12. **The seed edge's label `last_end`**: undefined anywhere; it blocks both a seeded build (item 2) and the comparison of a\n   257\t    proposed edge with a `supports` edge in the same reading direction (2.2). Returned to the council; its meaning is not\n   258\t    invented here.\n   259\t\n   260\t## 8. The first proof on stored data (`separation_proof.py`, `separated_edges.py`), $0\n   261\t\n   262\tThe seam replaces three functions of the store for the life of the proof (what judging reads, what a change queues, what\n   263\tfiling reads); no package file changes. 35 registered expectations hold; the script exits with an error otherwise.\n   264\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'p=docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness\nfor f in council-q/RESULT.md dependency-probe/{RESULT.md,claude-marks.md,render-check.json} dependency-design/{selection-census.md,selection_census.py}; do nl -ba \"$p/$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'p=docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness\nfor f in council-q/RESULT.md dependency-probe/{RESULT.md,claude-marks.md,render-check.json} dependency-design/{selection-census.md,selection_census.py}; do nl -ba \"$p/$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Council Q: would an analyst use poc-v118's library? (2026-11-20; consensus in round 2; $0)\n     2\t\n     3\tAlex, asked to read the served 09-20 library himself: \"dont' you take a look for me let the council tak ea crack at it\". Record:\n     4\t`brief.md`, `library-pack.md` (the served library as text), Codex round 1 `codex-r1.md` (thread `01a1fba6-8066-7031-9a26-da22b7dcbd3d`,\n     5\tread-only, memories off), Claude's read written before reading Codex's `claude-r1.md`, round 2 `round2-brief.md` and `codex-r2.md`.\n     6\tBoth readers are models; no person has read the output yet.\n     7\t\n     8\t## Answer\n     9\t**PARTLY.** An analyst would use it to find what happened, who said it and the source, and to check milestones. They would not use\n    10\tits number answers, because linked number questions contradict each other.\n    11\t\n    12\t## What both readers found\n    13\t- **Most useful:** the evidence is faithful. Every claim checked against its article was right (Claude 10, Codex 10; one Codex \"wrong\"\n    14\t  is the $12.9B Hugging Face total, which the 8-K splits as $11.9B plus up to $1.0B retention). Settled events are right on primary\n    15\t  sources: v4 (Vera Rubin production shipments began in August, earnings call and 10-Q), v34 (8-K: agreement signed 2026-09-02), v3.\n    16\t- **Most unsatisfying:** v9 (FY2027 Data Center revenue) carries Q4 Data Center at about $146B while v14 (FY2027 total revenue)\n    17\t  carries all of Q4 at about $127B. v9's \"market reference\" of $414B is its own estimate of 09-12/13 relabelled from 09-15, and on\n    18\t  09-20 it moved a band on a SemiAnalysis forecast dated 07-26. Also v8 against v16 (CPU), v35 against v14 (FY2028 base), and v21\n    19\t  applying the FY2028 70% outlook to FY2027.\n    20\t- **It recurs in all three draws** (read at $0 through `bundle.library` at 09-20): old-1 Data Center Q4 about $126B against a total\n    21\t  Q4 of about $112B; old-3 a total of about $446B against Data Center of about $375B (an 84% mix, weaker evidence).\n    22\t- **Mechanism:** no question has a dependency and no judgement cites another question (0 of 139 questions, 0 of 4,932 judgements\n    23\t  across the three draws). Each number question is judged alone. The missing piece is decided and not built: D368 item 2 (Alex,\n    24\t  2026-11-05: \"2. 做\"), D51 and D191 point 2; D368 item 4 sets the direction: the broader (root) question reads its narrower\n    25\t  drivers' conclusions.\n    26\t- **Also:** duplicates (v1/v2, v3/v22, v30/v31, v14/v21, v17/v35, v8/v16); small or non-NVIDIA bets (v11, v13, v19, v25, v26, v32,\n    27\t  v36, v38, v41, v42); missing bets (the next quarter, Blackwell volumes through the Rubin transition, AMD and customers' own chips,\n    28\t  China revenue); v6 overrides the $1.08T consensus with NVIDIA's own top-five $1.3T scaled to four; v5's figures are total capex\n    29\t  for a data-center-capex question; v15's band rests on an unsupported $28B; v43's Yes rests on the target close date; 4 of 25\n    30\t  sampled uncited evidence items worth keeping (Codex, one sample); the page opens on the newest questions.\n    31\t\n    32\t## Recommendation (consensus) and one test\n    33\tDo not build the daily wrapper yet: a daily refresh would deliver the same contradictions every day. First close the cross-question\n    34\tcontradiction with the decided dependencies. It changes frozen matching and judging, so it is a new version Alex opens.\n    35\t1. **$0 first:** render the exact judging request in D368's direction (the total-revenue question reading the Data Center\n    36\t   question's conclusion, `views/judging.py` `_lines_english`) and check that the decisive figures (Q4, the reference) survive the\n    37\t   600-character cut.\n    38\t2. **Then a few cents, only if they do:** re-judge the three draws' linked revenue questions at 09-20 with the dependency, three\n    39\t   samples each beside fresh unchanged controls. Pass: source-backed quarterly figures that reconcile (Data Center never above total).\n    40\t   Misleading if a forced mix or a later answer fed back manufactures agreement.\n     1\t# Dependency probe: does linking reconcile the Data Center answer with total revenue? (Alex's A, D691; 2026-11-20; $0.0176)\n     2\t\n     3\t**PASS by the registered rule** (`reading-rule.md`, fixed before any answer; council rounds 3–7 in this folder, thread\n     4\t`01a1fba6-8066-7031-9a26-da22b7dcbd3d`, consensus in rounds 6 and 7).\n     5\t\n     6\t## What was sent\n     7\told-2's v9 (FY2027 Data Center revenue) at 09-20, six fresh samples (`probe.py send`, `send.log`, `run/answers.jsonl`):\n     8\t- control: the stored request, rebuilt byte-equal through production code (`check.txt`);\n     9\t- changed: the same request plus v14's (FY2027 total revenue) 09-20 conclusion through one dependency, rendered by production code.\n    10\told-1 and old-3 were not sent: their rendered link loses the decisive figure at the 600-character cut (`render-check.json`).\n    11\t\n    12\t## Result\n    13\t| Arm | Reconcile (A–D all hold) | Q4 Data Center | Room left for the rest of NVIDIA in Q4 (midpoint) |\n    14\t|---|---|---|---|\n    15\t| changed | 2 of 3 (samples 1 and 3 used v14's $127B Q4 times the reported 92.5% mix; sample 2 only noticed v14) | $117.5B, $118B, $120–125B | $9.5B, $9B, $4.5B |\n    16\t| control | 0 of 3 (each sizes Q4 with an unquantified \"acceleration\") | $120–126B, $125–130B, $125–130B | $4B, −$0.5B, −$0.5B |\n    17\t\n    18\tClaude's marks (`claude-marks.md`, first) and a fresh blind Codex reader (`codex-blind.md`) agree on every answer; key outside the\n    19\trepo (`~/.hivemind-keys/dependency-probe-2026-11-20/key.json`). No refusal in either arm.\n    20\t\n    21\t## What it does not show\n    22\t- **The stored extreme did not recur.** All six answers are rung 3 at $382–394B; none repeats the stored rung 2, about $410B, Q4\n    23\t  $146B. Six samples leave that error's frequency unknown. The recurring problem is milder: a Q4 Data Center at or near the whole\n    24\t  company's Q4.\n    25\t- **Coherence is not accuracy:** the passing answers inherit v14's own assumption (Q4 at Q3's 12% growth) and carry the latest\n    26\t  segment share forward.\n    27\t- **One draw, one pair.** Linking was not tested in the root-reads-driver direction or on any other pair.\n    28\t- **The self-made reference is a separate live defect:** five of six answers still call the $414B (v9's own estimate of 09-12/13)\n    29\t  \"the market reference\", one of them a reconciling answer.\n    30\t\n    31\t## Next (registered, consensus)\n    32\tReopen the dependency design review at $0 with the council, then Alex: D542's five open items, the `reads`/`supports` separation, and\n    33\tD538's seven filing changes (a link can remove evidence from the company-wide question). Trace the self-made reference on stored\n    34\tanswers apart, at $0. The daily wrapper stays deferred; council Q's PARTLY stands.\n     1\t# Claude's marks, written before the blind reader (reading-rule.md, A to E)\n     2\t\n     3\tv14's 09-20 figures: FY $419B, Q3 $113.5B, Q4 $127B. Room floors (D): Data Center FY at most $394B, Q3 at most $107.5B, Q4 at most\n     4\t$121B. A stated range is read at its midpoint (the rule did not say; the lower-bound reading is given beside it and changes no verdict,\n     5\tbecause C fails those answers anyway).\n     6\t\n     7\t| Answer | A: FY / Q3 / Q4 | B adds up | C grounded | D room (midpoint) | E | Reconciles |\n     8\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n     9\t| control 1 | $389–394B / $100B / $125–130B | yes | no: Q4 is \"an allowance for the stated Q4 acceleration\", which it says is not quantified | Q4 $127.5B > $121B: no (lower bound $125B: no) | n/a | **no** |\n    10\t| control 2 | $389–394B / $99.9B / $125–130B | yes | no: same unquantified Q4 step | Q4 $127.5B: no (lower $125B: no) | n/a | **no** |\n    11\t| control 3 | $384–390B / $100B / $120–126B | yes | no: \"management's Q4 comments support\" a figure they never size | Q4 $123B: no (lower $120B: yes) | n/a | **no** |\n    12\t| changed 1 | $387–390B / $105B / $118B | yes (164 + 105 + 118 = 387) | yes: v14's Q3 $113.5B and Q4 $127B times the 92.5% mix in the request | FY room $30.5B, Q3 $8.5B, Q4 $9B: yes | used | **yes** |\n    13\t| changed 2 | $384–389B / $100B / $120–125B | yes | no: the same unquantified Q4 step as the controls | Q4 $122.5B: no (lower $120B: yes) | noticed (\"consistent with the dependent FY2027 total-revenue conclusion\") | **no** |\n    14\t| changed 3 | $382B / $99.9B / $117.5B | yes (164.25 + 99.9 + 117.5 = 381.65) | yes: v14's Q4 $127B times the 92.5% mix | FY room $37B, Q3 $13.6B, Q4 $9.5B: yes | used | **yes** |\n    15\t\n    16\t**Tally:** changed 2 of 3 reconcile, control 0 of 3, no refusal in either arm (all first attempts accepted). By the rule as written:\n    17\t**PASS**.\n    18\t\n    19\t**What else the answers show (not part of the rule):**\n    20\t- **The stored contradiction was partly one unlucky sample.** All six answers are rung 3 at about $382–394B. None repeats the stored\n    21\t  09-20 answer (rung 2, about $410B, Q4 $146B). The contradiction that remains in the controls is milder: Q4 Data Center of\n    22\t  $120–130B against a company-wide Q4 of $127B, leaving the rest of NVIDIA $0–7B where it earned $6.35B and $7.2B in Q1 and Q2.\n    23\t- **What the link changed:** two of three changed answers sized Q4 from v14's figures and the reported mix instead of an unsized\n    24\t  \"acceleration\". Changed 1 also rejected the self-made $414B reference and derived about $380B from the $411B consensus; the other\n    25\t  two kept \"$414B\" as the reference, as all three controls did.\n    26\t- **Coherence is not correctness:** the changed answers inherit v14's own assumption (Q4 at Q3's 12% growth).\n     1\t{\n     2\t \"old-1\": {\n     3\t  \"data_center\": \"v8\",\n     4\t  \"total\": \"v4\",\n     5\t  \"block_chars\": 640,\n     6\t  \"total_reasons_chars\": 533,\n     7\t  \"cut_at_600\": false,\n     8\t  \"shown\": \"- v4 (open; first_is_bullish) What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung 2 Bullish; reasons: The market reference is Wall Street consensus of about $411 billion, which is rung 2. NVIDIA's stated 70% full-year growth outlook implies about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion (rung 3), but the current-quarter guidance and the specific Q4 growth estimate imply a higher outcome: about $404 billion. That is above rung 3's $392 billion boundary but below rung 1's $431.2 billion threshold. Supply constraints and competition temper the upside; the evidence does not imply enough additional revenue to cross rung 1.\",\n     9\t  \"cut_off\": \"\"\n    10\t },\n    11\t \"old-2\": {\n    12\t  \"data_center\": \"v9\",\n    13\t  \"total\": \"v14\",\n    14\t  \"block_chars\": 714,\n    15\t  \"total_reasons_chars\": 755,\n    16\t  \"cut_at_600\": true,\n    17\t  \"shown\": \"- v14 (open; first_is_bullish) How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung 2 Bullish; reasons: The reference is about $411 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2; the cited Zacks growth estimate also implies about $405 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. The operating run-rate implies about $419 billion: estimate Q3 at roughly $113.5 billion by applying the cited average 3.1% beat to the $110.16 billion high end of guidance, then carry the guided 12% sequential growth forward to Q4, about $127 billion, alongside reported Q1 and Q2 revenue. Supply constraints are a meaningful downside risk, but the cited estimate of more than $100 billion in unfulfilled demand is for “next\",\n    18\t  \"cut_off\": \"year” and is not quantified as a reduction to FY2027 revenue. The implied outcome remains above $400 billion and below $450 billion, so it stays at rung 2.\"\n    19\t },\n    20\t \"old-3\": {\n    21\t  \"data_center\": \"v35\",\n    22\t  \"total\": \"v3\",\n    23\t  \"block_chars\": 722,\n    24\t  \"total_reasons_chars\": 1054,\n    25\t  \"cut_at_600\": true,\n    26\t  \"shown\": \"- v3 (open; first_is_bullish) What will NVIDIA report as total revenue for FY2027?: now rung 1 Strongly bullish; reasons: The market reference is about $411 billion (e4360, e5406), which sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). The evidence-based run-rate is about $419 billion before the additional second-half data-center-compute uplift: first-half revenue was $177.837 billion; Q3 is about $113.6 billion using the $108 billion midpoint guidance and the cited 3.1% average beat over the high end; carrying the guided 12% sequential growth pace forward gives about $127 billion for Q4. Applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% uplift only to second-half data-center compute adds about $27–28 billion, implying roughly $\",\n    27\t  \"cut_off\": \"446 billion and crossing the rung-1 boundary. The analyst view of 90% Q3 and 65% Q4 year-over-year growth is a counter-signal, but it does not outweigh the guided-growth run-rate and the specific second-half compute tracking. The 70% full-year growth statement would imply about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue, but that would require a sharp Q4 decline after the reported first half and Q3 guidance, so it is inconsistent with the nearer-term run-rate.\"\n    28\t }\n    29\t}\n     1\t# Selection census for the dependency design review (D692), $0\n     2\t\n     3\tGenerated by `selection_census.py` from the three finished poc-v118 draws (`poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-{1,2,3}`).\n     4\tNone of the three stores holds a dependency row (the Jev matcher returns `dependencies: []`, `jev_matching.py`).\n     5\t\n     6\t## old-1\n     7\t\n     8\t### The matcher's \"part_of\" answers (>= 0.5) on questions it opened as new: 20 openings\n     9\t\n    10\t| seq | opened | collision judge asked | part_of (other question, probability) |\n    11\t|---|---|---|---|\n    12\t| 8 | v2 \"What total amount of NVIDIA’s credit-support and capacity-pu\" | True | v1 0.77 \"How much credit support for AI clouds will NVIDIA have committed at th\" |\n    13\t| 62 | v8 \"How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for \" | False | v4 1.00 \"What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?\"; v5 0.70 \"What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2028?\" |\n    14\t| 265 | v15 \"How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\" | False | v4 0.99 \"What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?\"; v5 0.78 \"What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2028?\"; v8 0.70 \"How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\" |\n    15\t| 507 | v18 \"How much financing will NVIDIA have provided or committed to\" | True | v14 0.56 \"How much will NVIDIA’s supply-chain purchase commitments outstanding b\" |\n    16\t| 527 | v19 \"Will NVIDIA agree to provide the reported financing guarante\", v20 \"Will NVIDIA agree to finance OpenAI chip purchases for the s\" | True | v2 0.81 \"What total amount of NVIDIA’s credit-support and capacity-purchase obl\"; v1 0.76 \"How much credit support for AI clouds will NVIDIA have committed at th\" |\n    17\t| 527 | v19 \"Will NVIDIA agree to provide the reported financing guarante\", v20 \"Will NVIDIA agree to finance OpenAI chip purchases for the s\" | True | v1 0.74 \"How much credit support for AI clouds will NVIDIA have committed at th\"; v2 0.71 \"What total amount of NVIDIA’s credit-support and capacity-purchase obl\" |\n    18\t| 629 | v24 \"Will NVIDIA agree to provide a financing guarantee for OpenA\", v25 \"Will NVIDIA agree to finance OpenAI chip purchases for the s\" | True | v2 0.76 \"What total amount of NVIDIA’s credit-support and capacity-purchase obl\"; v1 0.74 \"How much credit support for AI clouds will NVIDIA have committed at th\" |\n    19\t| 629 | v24 \"Will NVIDIA agree to provide a financing guarantee for OpenA\", v25 \"Will NVIDIA agree to finance OpenAI chip purchases for the s\" | True | v1 0.65 \"How much credit support for AI clouds will NVIDIA have committed at th\"; v2 0.61 \"What total amount of NVIDIA’s credit-support and capacity-purchase obl\" |\n    20\t| 821 | v28 \"How much will NVIDIA’s committed equity-investment obligatio\" | True | v23 0.97 \"Will NVIDIA complete the reported $5 billion investment in Safe Superi\"; v17 0.73 \"Will NVIDIA complete its planned $1 billion investment in NAVER Corp. \"; v10 0.58 \"Will NVIDIA complete an investment in Firmus Technologies’ preference \" |\n    21\t| 883 | v30 \"Will NVIDIA complete its planned $1.5 billion investment in \" | True | v28 0.95 \"How much will NVIDIA’s committed equity-investment obligations to ecos\" |\n    22\t| 893 | v31 \"Will NVIDIA complete its planned $1.5 billion investment in \" | True | v28 0.90 \"How much will NVIDIA’s committed equity-investment obligations to ecos\" |\n    23\t| 967 | v33 \"Will NVIDIA complete its reported $1 billion investment in P\" | True | v28 0.91 \"How much will NVIDIA’s committed equity-investment obligations to ecos\" |\n    24\t| 1262 | v36 \"How much financing will NVIDIA have provided to MediaTek thr\" | True | v28 0.56 \"How much will NVIDIA’s committed equity-investment obligations to ecos\" |\n    25\t| 1267 | v37 \"Will NVIDIA complete its $3.5 billion investment in MediaTek\" | True | v28 0.93 \"How much will NVIDIA’s committed equity-investment obligations to ecos\" |\n    26\t| 1394 | v39 \"Will NVIDIA agree to invest in Thinking Machines Lab's repor\" | True | v28 0.82 \"How much will NVIDIA’s committed equity-investment obligations to ecos\" |\n    27\t| 1410 | v40 \"How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow year over year in \" | False | v8 0.79 \"How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\" |\n    28\t| 1482 | v42 \"How much Networking revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\" | True | v4 0.99 \"What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?\"; v8 0.90 \"How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\"; v5 0.75 \"What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2028?\"; v40 0.59 \"How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow year over year in FY2028?\" |\n    29\t| 1569 | v44 \"How much Vera CPU revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2028?\" | True | v5 0.96 \"What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2028?\"; v40 0.92 \"How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow year over year in FY2028?\"; v15 0.88 \"How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\"; v4 0.85 \"What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?\"; v8 0.56 \"How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\" |\n    30\t| 1661 | v45 \"Will NVIDIA complete an investment in Anthropic’s potential \" | True | v28 0.94 \"How much will NVIDIA’s committed equity-investment obligations to ecos\" |\n    31\t| 1664 | v46 \"Will NVIDIA agree to invest $10 billion in Anthropic’s IPO b\" | True | v28 0.93 \"How much will NVIDIA’s committed equity-investment obligations to ecos\" |\n    32\t\n    33\t### Number questions by typed key (measure, scope, period, unit): 19\n    34\t\n    35\t| question | measure | scope | period | unit | text |\n    36\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    37\t| v6 | spend_total | Data center capital expenditures of the four largest AI hyperscalers | 2026-01-01..2026-12-31 | USD | How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data center capital expenditures i |\n    38\t| v1 | financing_amount | AI-cloud credit support | 2026-02-01..2027-01-31 | USD | How much credit support for AI clouds 