{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'live_lock|writer_lock|BEGIN|begin immediate|config_checksum|last_written' scripts/replay.py src/hivemind/{replay,dispatch}.py src/hivemind/views/{round,writing,store}.py\nnl -ba src/hivemind/dispatch.py | sed -n '20,68p'\nnl -ba src/hivemind/views/store.py | sed -n '13,66p;100,125p;168,205p'\nrg -n 'cites_views|cited_views' src/hivemind/{bundle,service,idea}.py poc/ui/src\nrg -n 'lock|store' docs/prd/08-*.md | head -35\"","aggregated_output":"src/hivemind/views/writing.py:115:        conn.execute('begin immediate')\nsrc/hivemind/views/store.py:18:    'judgements': 'judgement_id integer primary key autoincrement, view_id text, seq integer, input_id text, rung integer, reasons text, cited_evidence_json text, cited_views_json text, pushes_json text, changed integer, why_changed text, config_checksum text, call_id integer, request_sha256 text, publication_day text, theme_pushes_json text, reference text, implied_outcome text',\nsrc/hivemind/views/store.py:221:    resolved, a store with another hard link refused (`live_lock.LockRefused`; live-service/OPERATING-CONTRACT.md, row 7), since\nsrc/hivemind/views/store.py:223:    from hivemind import live_lock\nsrc/hivemind/views/store.py:224:    real, _ = live_lock.own_path(path)\nsrc/hivemind/replay.py:64:    last_written = conn.execute('select max(publication_day) from processing_steps where sequence_no > 0').fetchone()[0]\nsrc/hivemind/replay.py:65:    if last_written and start < last_written:\nsrc/hivemind/replay.py:66:        raise BackdatingError(f'views database already holds rounds of {last_written}; a replay cannot start before it (D31)')\n    20\tSTEPS = ('dispatched', 'extracted', 'matched', 'written', 'judged', 'handed_over')\n    21\t\n    22\t\n    23\tdef route(conn, item, universe):\n    24\t    missing = [field for field in REQUIRED if not item.get(field)]\n    25\t    if missing:\n    26\t        store.pool(conn, 'incomplete_item', ','.join(missing), {'input_id': item.get('input_id'), 'missing': missing}, 0)\n    27\t        return Route('returned', reason=f'missing {missing}')\n    28\t    input_id = item['input_id']\n    29\t    if store.is_processed(conn, input_id):\n    30\t        return Route('skipped', reason='already processed')\n    31\t    last = store.last_step(conn, input_id)\n    32\t    if last and last['step'] in STEPS and last['sequence_no']:\n    33\t        # unfinished work keeps its sequence number: after an ok step it continues at the next step, after a\n    34\t        # failed step it retries that step (06-views: 失败从失败的那一步重试)\n    35\t        position = STEPS.index(last['step'])\n    36\t        resume_from = STEPS[position + 1] if last['result'] == 'ok' else last['step']\n    37\t        return Route('extract', sequence_no=last['sequence_no'], resume_from=resume_from, reason='resuming')\n    38\t    if item.get('copy_of'):\n    39\t        return Route('merged', reason=f'copy of {item[\"copy_of\"]}')\n    40\t    if item.get('supersedes'):\n    41\t        store.pool(conn, 'correction_not_supported', item['supersedes'], {'input_id': input_id}, 0)\n    42\t        return Route('not_processed', reason='correction (supersedes) not supported in the POC')\n    43\t    category = item['category']\n    44\t    objects = [o['value'] for o in item.get('object_identifiers') or [] if universe.has(o['value'])]\n    45\t    if not objects:\n    46\t        return Route('not_processed', reason='no object on the research list')\n    47\t    if not any(universe.watches(o, category) for o in objects):\n    48\t        return Route('not_processed', reason=f'category {category} not watched for {objects}')\n    49\t    # the sequence number is taken in arrival order and written at once, so two items routed before either is\n    50\t    # processed (a whole day is routed first, then run) never share a number\n    51\t    sequence_no = store.next_sequence(conn)\n    52\t    store.mark_step(conn, input_id, sequence_no, 'dispatched', 'ok', '', item.get('publication_day'), time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S'))\n    53\t    conn.commit()\n    54\t    return Route('extract', sequence_no=sequence_no, reason='')\n    55\t\n    56\t\n    57\tdef mark(conn, item, sequence_no, step, result='ok', reason=''):\n    58\t    store.mark_step(conn, item['input_id'], sequence_no, step, result, reason, item.get('publication_day'), time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S'))\n    13\tTABLES = {\n    14\t    'views': 'view_id text primary key, object_id text, object_kind text, kind text, looks_at text, question text, due_day text, year text, created_seq integer, created_from_input text, created_at text',\n    15\t    'rungs': 'view_id text, position integer, label text, condition text, lo real, hi real',\n    16\t    'view_keys': 'view_id text primary key, measure text, scope text, scope_key text, period_start text, period_end text, basis text, unit text, event_type text, seq integer, scope_source text, key_version integer',\n    17\t    'view_switches': 'id integer primary key autoincrement, view_id text, open integer, reason text, by_whom text, after_seq integer',\n    18\t    'judgements': 'judgement_id integer primary key autoincrement, view_id text, seq integer, input_id text, rung integer, reasons text, cited_evidence_json text, cited_views_json text, pushes_json text, changed integer, why_changed text, config_checksum text, call_id integer, request_sha256 text, publication_day text, theme_pushes_json text, reference text, implied_outcome text',\n    19\t    'evidence': 'evidence_id text primary key, quote text, one_line text, speaker text, original_who text, original_day text, tier text, event_time text, created_seq integer, created_from_input text',\n    20\t    'evidence_sources': 'id integer primary key autoincrement, evidence_id text, input_id text, category text, publication_day text, location text, seq integer',\n    21\t    'evidence_links': 'id integer primary key autoincrement, evidence_id text, view_id text, seq integer',\n    22\t    'replacements': 'id integer primary key autoincrement, old_evidence_id text, new_evidence_id text, said_day text, seq integer',   # history: written before D202, read as before\n    23\t    'revisions': 'id integer primary key autoincrement, old_evidence_id text, new_evidence_id text, said_day text, seq integer',      # D202: the new piece revises the old; both stay in force\n    24\t    'same_claims': 'id integer primary key autoincrement, old_evidence_id text, new_evidence_id text, seq integer',                    # D204: the matcher read the new piece as the same claim as the old; both stored, both in force\n    25\t    'dependencies': 'dependency_id integer primary key autoincrement, view_id text, depends_on text, wait_chain_view_id text, wait_year text, direction text, seq integer',\n    26\t    'dependency_resolutions': 'id integer primary key autoincrement, dependency_id integer, resolved_to text, reason text, seq integer',\n    27\t    'successions': 'id integer primary key autoincrement, view_id text, succeeds text, seq integer',\n    28\t    'handovers': 'id integer primary key autoincrement, input_id text, seq integer, handover_json text',\n    29\t    'match_records': 'id integer primary key autoincrement, seq integer, input_id text, call_no integer, shown_json text, request_sha256 text, call_id integer, decisions_json text, checks_json text, written_json text',\n    30\t    'processing_steps': 'id integer primary key autoincrement, input_id text, sequence_no integer, step text, result text, reason text, publication_day text, at text',\n    31\t    'review_pool_entries': 'id integer primary key autoincrement, kind text, key text, example_json text, seq integer',\n    32\t    'late_statements': 'id integer primary key autoincrement, input_id text, evidence_quote text, reason text, seq integer',\n    33\t    'days_completed': 'id integer primary key autoincrement, day text, seq integer, at text',\n    34\t    # D149 research groups: an ongoing business subject several distinct theses belong to; membership is decided by extraction and written with the thesis\n    35\t    'thesis_groups': 'group_id text primary key, name text, scope text, aliases text, created_seq integer, created_from_input text',\n    36\t    'thesis_group_members': 'id integer primary key autoincrement, group_id text, view_id text, seq integer, input_id text, basis text',\n    37\t    # D166 (2026-11-01): the names a later declaration gives a stored group, appended once each; a group's aliases are its creation's plus these\n    38\t    'thesis_group_aliases': 'id integer primary key autoincrement, group_id text, alias text, seq integer, input_id text',\n    39\t}\n    40\t\n    41\tINDEXES = [\n    42\t    'create index if not exists views_object on views(object_id)',\n    43\t    'create index if not exists judgements_view on judgements(view_id, judgement_id)',\n    44\t    # 06-views step 7: a view is judged once per round (one item = one sequence number)\n    45\t    'create unique index if not exists judgements_once_per_round on judgements(view_id, seq)',\n    46\t    'create index if not exists links_view on evidence_links(view_id)',\n    47\t    'create index if not exists links_evidence on evidence_links(evidence_id)',\n    48\t    'create index if not exists sources_evidence on evidence_sources(evidence_id)',\n    49\t    'create index if not exists steps_input on processing_steps(input_id, id)',\n    50\t    'create index if not exists switches_view on view_switches(view_id, id)',\n    51\t    'create index if not exists deps_view on dependencies(view_id)',\n    52\t]\n    53\t\n    54\t\n    55\tdef open_db(path):\n    56\t    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(path)), exist_ok=True)\n    57\t    conn = sqlite3.connect(path)\n    58\t    conn.row_factory = sqlite3.Row\n    59\t    for name, columns in TABLES.items():\n    60\t        conn.execute(f'create table if not exists {name} ({columns})')\n    61\t        conn.execute(f\"create trigger if not exists {name}_no_update before update on {name} begin select raise(abort, 'append-only'); end\")\n    62\t        conn.execute(f\"create trigger if not exists {name}_no_delete before delete on {name} begin select raise(abort, 'append-only'); end\")\n    63\t    for statement in INDEXES:\n    64\t        conn.execute(statement)\n    65\t    ensure_writable_columns(conn)\n    66\t    conn.execute(\"create virtual table if not exists views_fts using fts5(view_id unindexed, text, tokenize='trigram')\")\n   100\t                 **({'lo': r.get('lo'), 'hi': r.get('hi')} if has_bounds else {})) for r in rows]\n   101\t\n   102\t\n   103\tdef add_key(conn, view_id, key, seq):\n   104\t    \"\"\"The view's key (keyed-views-plan.md 2.1), written once with the view.\"\"\"\n   105\t    from .. import universe as _universe\n   106\t    from .identity import is_shared\n   107\t    scope_source = 'shared' if is_shared(key) else key.get('scope_source', 'model')      # D197: an aggregate's scope is wording\n   108\t    insert(conn, 'view_keys', view_id=view_id, measure=key['measure'], scope=key.get('scope', ''), scope_key=_universe.scope_key(key.get('scope', '')),\n   109\t           period_start=key.get('period_start', ''), period_end=key.get('period_end', ''), basis=key.get('basis', 'n/a'), unit=key.get('unit', 'n/a'),\n   110\t           event_type=key.get('event_type', 'n/a'), seq=seq, scope_source=scope_source, key_version=key.get('key_version'))\n   111\t\n   112\t\n   113\tdef ensure_key_columns(conn):\n   114\t    \"\"\"A keyed ledger written before `scope_source` gains the column (append-only forbids updates, not new columns); its\n   115\t    earlier keys read as model-owned scopes unless the scope is empty.\"\"\"\n   116\t    columns = [r[1] for r in conn.execute('pragma table_info(view_keys)')]\n   117\t    if columns and 'scope_source' not in columns:\n   118\t        conn.execute('alter table view_keys add column scope_source text')\n   119\t    if columns and 'key_version' not in columns:\n   120\t        conn.execute('alter table view_keys add column key_version integer')\n   121\t\n   122\t\n   123\tdef ensure_group_tables(conn):\n   124\t    \"\"\"A ledger written before research groups (D165) gains the group tables at its first write; nothing else changes.\"\"\"\n   125\t    for name in ('thesis_groups', 'thesis_group_members', 'thesis_group_aliases'):\n   168\t    \"\"\"Open views of the object with the same measure, scope and unit whose period overlaps: the ones a candidate must\n   169\t    be confirmed against before it may open (keyed-views-plan.md 2.5). A basis or unit of n/a on either side matches\n   170\t    any; GAAP and non-GAAP are two quantities, and so are a level in USD and a growth rate in percent.\"\"\"\n   171\t    rows = [dict(r) for r in conn.execute('''select k.* from view_keys k join views v on v.view_id=k.view_id\n   172\t                                            where v.object_id=? and k.measure=? order by v.rowid''', (object_id, measure))]\n   173\t    return [r['view_id'] for r in rows if is_open(conn, r['view_id'])\n   174\t            and (event_type is None or r['event_type'] == event_type)\n   175\t            and (basis == 'n/a' or r['basis'] == 'n/a' or r['basis'] == basis)\n   176\t            and (unit == 'n/a' or r['unit'] == 'n/a' or r['unit'] == unit)\n   177\t            and (r.get('key_version') != 2 or ((scope_key is None or r['scope_key'] == scope_key)\n   178\t                 and r['period_start'] <= (period_end or '9999') and r['period_end'] >= (period_start or '0000')))]\n   179\t\n   180\t\n   181\tdef exact_key_views(conn, candidate):\n   182\t    \"\"\"All open exact identities, independent of a search limit; legacy keys are never automatic identities.\"\"\"\n   183\t    from .identity import automatic_key\n   184\t    target = automatic_key(candidate)\n   185\t    if target is None:\n   186\t        return []\n   187\t    rows = conn.execute('''select k.*, v.object_id from view_keys k join views v on v.view_id=k.view_id\n   188\t                           where v.object_id=? and k.measure=?''', (candidate['object'], candidate['measure']))\n   189\t    return [r['view_id'] for r in rows if automatic_key(dict(r), r['object_id']) == target and is_open(conn, r['view_id'])]\n   190\t\n   191\t\n   192\tdef is_open(conn, view_id):\n   193\t    row = conn.execute('select open from view_switches where view_id=? order by id desc limit 1', (view_id,)).fetchone()\n   194\t    return bool(row and row['open'])\n   195\t\n   196\t\n   197\tdef close_view(conn, view_id, reason, by_whom, seq):\n   198\t    insert(conn, 'view_switches', view_id=view_id, open=0, reason=reason, by_whom=by_whom, after_seq=seq)\n   199\t\n   200\t\n   201\tdef open_views(conn):\n   202\t    rows = conn.execute('select view_id from views').fetchall()\n   203\t    return [r['view_id'] for r in rows if is_open(conn, r['view_id'])]\n   204\t\n   205\t\nsrc/hivemind/bundle.py:122:                           'cited_views': json.loads(j['cited_views_json'] or '[]'), 'pushes': json.loads(j['pushes_json'] or '[]'),\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c '\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport sqlite3,json\np=Path(\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-2/prod\\\")\ndef db(f):\n return sqlite3.connect(f.resolve().as_uri()+\\\"?mode=ro&immutable=1\\\",uri=True)\nwith db(p/\\\"views.sqlite\\\") as c, db(p/\\\"archive.sqlite\\\") as a:\n print(c.execute(\\\"select view_id,kind,object_kind,created_seq,created_from_input from views where created_seq between 148 and 186 order by created_seq\\\").fetchall())\n for row in c.execute(\\\"select evidence_id,speaker,original_day,quote,one_line from evidence where evidence_id in ('\\\\''e195'\\\\'','\\\\''e202'\\\\'','\\\\''e74'\\\\'','\\\\''e170'\\\\'','\\\\''e171'\\\\'','\\\\''e216'\\\\'','\\\\''e217'\\\\'','\\\\''e162'\\\\'','\\\\''e163'\\\\'','\\\\''e153'\\\\'','\\\\''e25'\\\\'','\\\\''e89'\\\\'','\\\\''e190'\\\\'','\\\\''e204'\\\\'','\\\\''e159'\\\\'') order by rowid\\\"):\n  print(*row)\n for row in c.execute(\\\"select dependency_id from dependencies\\\"): print(row)\n for r in c.execute(\\\"select j.view_id,j.publication_day,j.call_id from judgements j where j.view_id in ('\\\\''v9'\\\\'','\\\\''v14'\\\\'') and j.publication_day between '\\\\''2026-07-10'\\\\'' and '\\\\''2026-07-12'\\\\'' order by judgement_id\\\"):\n  req,ans=a.execute(\\\"select request_json,answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?\\\", (r[2],)).fetchone()\n  print(r, len(req),{k:v for k,v in json.loads(ans).items() if k in ('\\\\''rung'\\\\'','\\\\''reference'\\\\'','\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\'','\\\\''changed'\\\\'','\\\\''why_changed'\\\\'')})\nfor line in (Path(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-probe/run/answers.jsonl\\\")).read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n r=d[\\\"attempts\\\"][-1]\n print(d[\\\"arm\\\"],d[\\\"sample\\\"],r[\\\"input_tokens\\\"],r[\\\"output_tokens\\\"], r[\\\"answer\\\"][\\\"implied_outcome\\\"])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c '\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport sqlite3,json\np=Path(\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-2/prod\\\")\ndef db(f):\n return sqlite3.connect(f.resolve().as_uri()+\\\"?mode=ro&immutable=1\\\",uri=True)\nwith db(p/\\\"views.sqlite\\\") as c, db(p/\\\"archive.sqlite\\\") as a:\n print(c.execute(\\\"select view_id,kind,object_kind,created_seq,created_from_input from views where created_seq between 148 and 186 order by created_seq\\\").fetchall())\n for row in c.execute(\\\"select evidence_id,speaker,original_day,quote,one_line from evidence where evidence_id in ('\\\\''e195'\\\\'','\\\\''e202'\\\\'','\\\\''e74'\\\\'','\\\\''e170'\\\\'','\\\\''e171'\\\\'','\\\\''e216'\\\\'','\\\\''e217'\\\\'','\\\\''e162'\\\\'','\\\\''e163'\\\\'','\\\\''e153'\\\\'','\\\\''e25'\\\\'','\\\\''e89'\\\\'','\\\\''e190'\\\\'','\\\\''e204'\\\\'','\\\\''e159'\\\\'') order by rowid\\\"):\n  print(*row)\n for row in c.execute(\\\"select dependency_id from dependencies\\\"): print(row)\n for r in c.execute(\\\"select j.view_id,j.publication_day,j.call_id from judgements j where j.view_id in ('\\\\''v9'\\\\'','\\\\''v14'\\\\'') and j.publication_day between '\\\\''2026-07-10'\\\\'' and '\\\\''2026-07-12'\\\\'' order by judgement_id\\\"):\n  req,ans=a.execute(\\\"select request_json,answer_json from model_calls where call_id=?\\\", (r[2],)).fetchone()\n  print(r, len(req),{k:v for k,v in json.loads(ans).items() if k in ('\\\\''rung'\\\\'','\\\\''reference'\\\\'','\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\'','\\\\''changed'\\\\'','\\\\''why_changed'\\\\'')})\nfor line in (Path(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-probe/run/answers.jsonl\\\")).read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n r=d[\\\"attempts\\\"][-1]\n print(d[\\\"arm\\\"],d[\\\"sample\\\"],r[\\\"input_tokens\\\"],r[\\\"output_tokens\\\"], r[\\\"answer\\\"][\\\"implied_outcome\\\"])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"[('v14', 'asset', 'company', 148, 'gnews:49f225cd79fb22132f3b9ee4')]\ne25 fool.com author Justin Pope 2026-07-02 Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year. The author said Vera Rubin was in full production and could begin shipping later in 2026.\ne74 tradingview.com  During the first quarter of fiscal 2027, the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenues, accounting for 92% of total sales. Revenues from the segment surged 92% year over year and grew 21% sequentially, driven by the rapid ramp-up of Blackwell 300 systems and robust networking demand. NVIDIA’s Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenue in FY2027 Q1, equal to 92% of total sales, up 92% year over year and 21% sequentially.\ne89 Investing.com, quoting an NVIDIA spokesperson  An Nvidia spokesperson swiftly refuted such claims in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, stating: \"Our roadmap is intact.\" An NVIDIA spokesperson said the company's product roadmap was intact, refuting claims of delays to Kyber NVL144.\ne153 Bloomberg, reporting the Bloomberg Intelligence survey 2026-07-07 Executives in the country say they’ll allocate 46% of their budget for artificial intelligence accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday. Chinese company executives surveyed by Bloomberg Intelligence said they plan to allocate 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% currently.\ne159 Bloomberg, reporting the survey report 2026-07-07 But a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap the growth of Chinese AI firms like Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corp. with ChangXin Memory Technologies Inc. poised to reap the benefits. The bottlenecks are shifting from sheer computing power to securing supply of high-bandwidth memory chips that support rapid data transfer, according to the report. The report said a global memory-chip shortage is likely to constrain Chinese AI firms’ growth, with high-bandwidth memory supply emerging as a bottleneck and ChangXin Memory Technologies poised to benefit.\ne162 moomoo.com  The report also says Nvidia’s hyperscaler sales rose 115% year over year, nearly twice cloud capex growth, suggesting wallet-share gains rather than share loss. Bank of America reported that NVIDIA’s sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year over year, nearly twice the growth in cloud capital expenditures.\ne163 moomoo.com  BofA still models Nvidia holding roughly 65% to 70% plus of AI capex over time, with the rest split across ASICs, AMD and other merchant vendors. Bank of America modeled NVIDIA retaining roughly 65% to 70% or more of AI capital expenditures over time, with the remainder going to ASICs, AMD and other merchant vendors.\ne170 fool.com (Adam Spatacco)  management guided for further revenue growth acceleration for the second quarter. NVIDIA management guided to accelerating Data Center segment revenue growth in the second quarter; no amount is stated.\ne171 fool.com (Adam Spatacco)  Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers. NVIDIA management cited roughly $1 trillion of revenue visibility for Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across calendar 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\ne190 moomoo.com  Quanta, which supplies AI servers powered by Nvidia’s GPUs, saw its monthly sales more than double to 385.19 billion New Taiwanese dollars in June from a year earlier, according to data compiled by Bloomberg. Quanta's monthly sales rose to NT$385.19 billion in June 2026, more than doubling year over year, according to Bloomberg data compiled for the item.\ne195 Jeran Wittenstein, citing data compiled by Bloomberg  The chipmaker is projected to deliver $228 billion in profits on sales of $393 billion in fiscal 2027, which ends Jan. 31. That would represent an expansion of 90% and 82%, respectively, according to data compiled by Bloomberg. Bloomberg data project NVIDIA FY2027 sales of $393 billion, up 82%, and profits of $228 billion, up 90%.\ne202 247wallst.com  Q2 FY27 guidance points to $91 billion in revenue. NVIDIA guided to total revenue of $91 billion for Q2 FY2027.\ne204 David Moadel, citing NVIDIA’s reported results  The company reported record revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, with Data Center revenue reaching $75.2 billion. NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, including Data Center revenue of $75.2 billion.\ne216 qz.com  Nvidia 's share exceeded 90% in training in 2025. Silicon Analysts estimated that NVIDIA held more than 90% of the AI-chip training market in 2025.\ne217 qz.com  Its inference share sat between 60% and 75% over the same period, undercut by growing competition from custom silicon. Silicon Analysts estimated NVIDIA’s AI-chip inference market share at 60%–75% in 2025, citing competition from custom silicon.\n('v14', '2026-07-10', 439) 9236 {'rung': 3, 'changed': True, 'why_changed': 'This is the first judgement.', 'reference': 'Bloomberg-compiled projections expect FY2027 sales of $393 billion, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).', 'implied_outcome': 'About $393 billion for FY2027, using Bloomberg’s direct sales projection; this is below $400 billion and therefore falls in rung 3.'}\n('v9', '2026-07-10', 444) 43269 {'rung': 3, 'changed': True, 'why_changed': 'The rung changes from 2 to 3, and the implied FY2027 figure changes from the previous roughly $420 billion estimate to roughly $357 billion. The new $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guidance constrains the prior extrapolation of 21% sequential Data Center growth; applying the Q1 segment mix and carrying forward the growth rate implied by total-revenue guidance produces an estimate below $400 billion.', 'reference': 'No FY2027 Data Center consensus or segment guidance is supplied, so there is no direct reference rung. The nearest company reference is $91 billion of total revenue guidance for Q2 FY2027, which is not itself an FY2027 Data Center figure and cannot be assigned a rung on this ladder.', 'implied_outcome': 'About $357 billion for FY2027: apply the Q1 Data Center share of 92% to the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide to estimate roughly $83.8 billion of Q2 Data Center revenue, then carry forward the 11.5% sequential growth implied by total-revenue guidance for the remaining quarters.'}\n('v14', '2026-07-11', 455) 9931 {'rung': 3, 'changed': False, 'why_changed': 'The implied figure and rung remain $393 billion and rung 3, respectively. The demand-mix observation adds qualitative support but gives no period-specific revenue change; the themes were already described as supportive in the previous conclusion.', 'reference': 'Bloomberg-compiled FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion; this market reference sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).', 'implied_outcome': \"About $393 billion for FY2027, using Bloomberg's specific projection. The AI-lab demand share and supportive capex and export-policy themes add upside pressure, but state no FY2027 NVIDIA revenue adjustment, so they do not imply a figure above $400 billion.\"}\n('v14', '2026-07-12', 517) 21147 {'rung': 3, 'changed': True, 'why_changed': 'The rung remains 3, but new Q1 reported revenue and Q2 guidance change the key supporting forecast: carrying the guided sequential growth forward implies about $387 billion, rather than relying on the prior $393 billion Bloomberg projection alone.', 'reference': \"Bloomberg's FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion is the specific market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion), $7 billion short of rung 2.\", 'implied_outcome': 'About $387 billion: starting with Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of approximately $91 billion, I carry the roughly 11.5% sequential increase forward to Q3 and Q4. This remains within rung 3 and below $400 billion.'}\n('v9', '2026-07-12', 520) 47704 {'rung': 2, 'changed': True, 'why_changed': 'The new management statement of roughly $1T in Blackwell and Vera Rubin revenue visibility, together with the Q2 growth-acceleration statement, changes the implied FY2027 outcome from the prior roughly $357B run-rate estimate to about $400–410B, crossing into rung 2.', 'reference': \"No FY2027 Data Center consensus or segment guidance is supplied. The closest company reference is NVIDIA's $91B Q2 FY2027 total-revenue guidance, which, using Q1 Data Center's approximately 92% share and carrying forward the guide-implied sequential growth, corresponds to about $357B for FY2027—rung 3.\", 'implied_outcome': 'About $400–410B of FY2027 Data Center revenue. I use the Q1 $75.25B result and Q2 growth outlook as the operating base, then give partial—not full—weight to the roughly $1T Blackwell/Vera Rubin visibility across calendar 2026–27 because it spans multiple periods and products; the discounted estimate is just above the $400B rung boundary.'}\ncontrol 1 18361 2761 About $389–394 billion: $164.25 billion reported in Q1–Q2, approximately $100 billion implied for Q3 from the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and roughly $125–130 billion for Q4 by carrying forward guided growth while allowing for the stated Q4 acceleration as Rubin supply builds.\nchanged 1 18541 5353 About $387–390 billion: H1 Data Center revenue was about $164 billion; applying the latest roughly 92.5% Data Center mix to v14's estimated Q3 total revenue of about $113.5 billion and Q4 total revenue of about $127 billion gives roughly $105 billion in Q3 and $118 billion in Q4. The result is below $400 billion and above $350 billion.\ncontrol 2 18361 4014 About $389–394 billion: $164.25 billion reported in Q1–Q2, approximately $99.9 billion for Q3 using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, plus an estimated $125–130 billion in Q4 as hyperscale growth reaccelerates and Rubin supply builds.\nchanged 2 18541 6586 About $384–389 billion: the first two quarters total about $164 billion, Q3 is approximately $100 billion using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and Q4 is estimated at roughly $120–125 billion as hyperscale growth reaccelerates and Rubin supply builds.\ncontrol 3 18361 4717 About $384–390 billion: approximately $164 billion in the first half, about $100 billion in Q3 from the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent 92.5% Data Center mix, plus roughly $120–126 billion in Q4 as supply builds and hyperscale growth reaccelerates.\nchanged 3 18541 3197 Approximately $382 billion: $164.25 billion reported in the first half, about $99.9 billion implied for Q3, and about $117.5 billion for Q4 by applying the recent 92.5% Data Center mix to v14’s roughly $127 billion Q4 total-revenue estimate.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"agent_message","text":"I found a case that could test the missing behavior through the replay. In the stored July 12 run, Data Center was forecast at $400–410B against about $387B for all of NVIDIA. The test would start when total revenue opened on July 10 and carry each version’s own answers forward."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'live_lock|one.*writer|two.*writer' scripts src/hivemind docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service --glob '*.py' --glob 'OPERATING-CONTRACT.md' | head -45\nnl -ba docs/DECISIONS.md | sed -n '216,248p'\nnl -ba src/hivemind/config.py | sed -n '20,122p'\nrg -n 'kind.*theme|KIND' src/hivemind/extraction.py src/hivemind/vocabulary.py | head -22\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'live_lock|one.*writer|two.*writer' scripts src/hivemind docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service --glob '*.py' --glob 'OPERATING-CONTRACT.md' | head -45\nnl -ba docs/DECISIONS.md | sed -n '216,248p'\nnl -ba src/hivemind/config.py | sed -n '20,122p'\nrg -n 'kind.*theme|KIND' src/hivemind/extraction.py src/hivemind/vocabulary.py | head -22\"","aggregated_output":"scripts/preflight.py:32:from hivemind import config, extraction, live_lock, model, testdb, universe, vocabulary  # noqa: E402\nscripts/preflight.py:187:    held = live_lock.acquire(path)\nscripts/preflight.py:191:            live_lock.verify(held)\nscripts/preflight.py:195:                live_lock.leave_wal_mode(target)\nscripts/preflight.py:200:        live_lock.verify(held)\nscripts/preflight.py:207:        live_lock.release(held)\nscripts/hivemind_api.py:34:from hivemind import bundle, config, idea, live_lock, model, search, service  # noqa: E402\nscripts/hivemind_api.py:41:REQUEST_ERRORS = (KeyError, service.LedgerError, live_lock.LockRefused, model.TransportError, model.SchemaError, config.ConfigError, ValueError, TypeError)\nscripts/hivemind_api.py:54:    if isinstance(error, (service.LedgerError, live_lock.LockRefused)):     # the store cannot be served as configured (row 7)\nscripts/hivemind_api.py:63:    is pinned by BEGIN and a first read, so every read of the request sees one state while a writer commits (live-service/\nscripts/snapshot_store.py:11:from hivemind import live_lock  # noqa: E402\nscripts/snapshot_store.py:19:        destination = live_lock.snapshot(arguments[0], arguments[1])\nsrc/hivemind/views/store.py:221:    resolved, a store with another hard link refused (`live_lock.LockRefused`; live-service/OPERATING-CONTRACT.md, row 7), since\nsrc/hivemind/views/store.py:223:    from hivemind import live_lock\nsrc/hivemind/views/store.py:224:    real, _ = live_lock.own_path(path)\nsrc/hivemind/parallel_day.py:1:\"\"\"One day's items with concurrent request preparation and one ordered writer (D172, D181).\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/filesystem_full_proof.py:29:from hivemind import live_lock, service  # noqa: E402\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/filesystem_full_proof.py:37:from hivemind import jev, live_lock, model\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/filesystem_full_proof.py:38:live_lock.LOCK_FOLDER = locks\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/filesystem_full_proof.py:71:        live_lock.LOCK_FOLDER = str(locks)\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/filesystem_full_proof.py:72:        held = live_lock.acquire(store)\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/filesystem_full_proof.py:73:        live_lock.release(held)\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/proof10_serving_while_writing.py:4:judge request answered from the stored archive by identity (a request with none stops the run). The writer is the built `replay`\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/proof10_serving_while_writing.py:35:from hivemind import live_lock, model, service  # noqa: E402\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/proof10_serving_while_writing.py:141:            live_lock.release(live_lock.acquire(store))\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/proof10_serving_while_writing.py:143:        except live_lock.LockRefused:\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/proof10_serving_while_writing.py:148:        except live_lock.LockRefused as refused:\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/proof10_serving_while_writing.py:184:            live_lock.release(live_lock.acquire(store))\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/proof10_serving_while_writing.py:186:        except live_lock.LockRefused:\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/OPERATING-CONTRACT.md:156:     - One function (`live_lock.lock_folder()`) gives the folder to both the writer and preflight: `~/.hivemind-locks/`,\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/OPERATING-CONTRACT.md:195:   - **Snapshots:** the snapshot script is the one toucher that copies a store while its writer runs, with the backup API.\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/OPERATING-CONTRACT.md:237:   - two overlapping MCP calls across a writer's commit each return their own transaction's state, two overlapping **search**\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/OPERATING-CONTRACT.md:463:**The snapshot helper** (`live_lock.snapshot(store_path, destination)`, used by `scripts/snapshot_store.py`, its user-facing entry\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/OPERATING-CONTRACT.md:501:  read-write one and the mode is still WAL. In two direct checks the writer itself did not leave it: the side files remained after\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/OPERATING-CONTRACT.md:525:  resolves a symlink and refuses a store with another hard link (`live_lock.LockRefused`, naming the reason).\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/OPERATING-CONTRACT.md:586:   - `live_lock.acquire` is refused, and so is preflight's `store_copies`, which names the snapshot route;\n   216\t- Jev is the matching engine (D169); its output is an opinion with a score, not the decision to keep or merge (D201). Code owns identity\n   217\t  on typed keys: a complete key equal, event type included (D154 *(Claude)*, D155, D164); a shared quantity is one question per theme,\n   218\t  measure and period, decided by code before any judge (D197); identity over a model-written scope stays a judgement (D167). When a\n   219\t  candidate's list holds a question of its own object and measure, the collision judge answers the analyst's question and its verdict\n   220\t  replaces Jev's (D190, D258).\n   221\t- A new thesis's dependencies are chosen in matching (D51); the default Jev path does not ask for them, so a thesis opened from an article\n   222\t  has none of its own *(to be built, D368)*; a later item may add a dependency to an existing thesis (D191 point 2) *(to be built, D368)*.\n   223\t- Every match writes a matching record, kept for human labels and a later smaller model (D40).\n   224\t\n   225\t**Judging**\n   226\t- A question is re-judged when what it reads changed: new or newly linked evidence, a dependency whose conclusion changed, a changed theme\n   227\t  (D51, D191, D195); daily, in one round (D63).\n   228\t- A judgement is a forecast against a reference (D260 *(Claude)*, D263): the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due day,\n   229\t  against consensus, guidance or the stated plan; \"not established\" is never a reason to stay; the answer states its reference and implied\n   230\t  outcome; a gap to the frozen judgement gold is closed in the judge's rule.\n   231\t- A judgement reads the evidence in view (D198): the arrivals since its fifth most recent judgement and the basis its last ten cited; the\n   232\t  rest stays in force.\n   233\t- A judgement may cite the questions it depends on, checked by code against its dependencies; evidence a candidate carries is compared\n   234\t  after the candidate's place is known (D47 *(Claude)*).\n   235\t- The judging call's output budget is 16,000 tokens (D205).\n   236\t- **Frozen** (D354): the collision judge (`collision-v2`), the judge (`judging-v7`), the matching prompt (`matching-v3`), the judge's\n   237\t  answer schema and the three Jev questions, pinned by `tests/test_frozen_questions_and_judgement.py`.\n   238\t\n   239\t## 8. Closing and pull mode (PRD 07, PRD 08)\n   240\t\n   241\t- At its due day a question freezes at the rung it stands on and closes; that rung is its conclusion; nothing waits for the result (D54,\n   242\t  D356).\n   243\t- Closing belongs to pull mode, at the end of each day; there is no separate final verdict and no day-end re-judging without new\n   244\t  evidence; a due day falls on or after the day the result is published, set when the question opens; the closing record names the frozen\n   245\t  rung or \"no data to verify\", is shown from the closing day and never gates; a closing on a day without items gets its own sequence\n   246\t  number (D356).\n   247\t- A question that depends on a closed one reads its frozen state and reasons (D54, D57).\n   248\t- Pull mode does nothing else at day end: D248 boundary 2's step that opened the next-deadline successor of an event question falling due\n    20\tdef _prompt_files(extraction, matching, rejudging, idea=None, idea_summary=None, collision=None, placement=None):\n    21\t    \"\"\"03-config: a version that names a prompt that does not exist is refused whole. An extraction setting's\n    22\t    prompt_version is '<category>-<v>': block 2 and 4 come from categories-<v>.json (the category's entry) and the\n    23\t    role from role-<v>.txt; matching, re-judging and the idea reading name their file without the extension.\"\"\"\n    24\t    for category, setting in extraction.items():\n    25\t        prefix, _, version = setting.prompt_version.rpartition('-')\n    26\t        if prefix != category or not version:\n    27\t            raise ConfigError(f'extraction.{category}: prompt_version {setting.prompt_version!r} must be {category}-<version>')\n    28\t        categories_file = prompt_path(f'categories-{version}.json')\n    29\t        if not os.path.exists(categories_file) or not os.path.exists(prompt_path(f'role-{version}.txt')):\n    30\t            raise ConfigError(f'extraction.{category}: prompts for version {version!r} do not exist')\n    31\t        with open(categories_file, encoding='utf-8') as handle:\n    32\t            if category not in json.load(handle):\n    33\t                raise ConfigError(f'extraction.{category}: categories-{version}.json has no entry for {category}')\n    34\t    for setting in (matching, rejudging, idea, idea_summary, collision, placement):\n    35\t        if setting is not None and not os.path.exists(prompt_path(setting.prompt_version + '.txt')):\n    36\t            raise ConfigError(f'{setting.name}: prompt {setting.prompt_version}.txt does not exist')\n    37\t\n    38\t\n    39\t@dataclasses.dataclass(frozen=True)\n    40\tclass Setting:\n    41\t    name: str\n    42\t    model: str\n    43\t    prompt_version: str\n    44\t    reasoning_effort: str\n    45\t    max_output_tokens: int\n    46\t    engine: str = 'llm'       # matching only: 'llm' (the generative call) or 'jev' (TypeSafe Jev, views/jev_matching.py)\n    47\t\n    48\t\n    49\tENGINES = ('llm', 'jev')\n    50\t\n    51\t\n    52\t@dataclasses.dataclass(frozen=True)\n    53\tclass Config:\n    54\t    version: str\n    55\t    checksum: str\n    56\t    provider: str\n    57\t    search_top_n: int\n    58\t    extraction: dict          # category -> Setting\n    59\t    matching: Setting\n    60\t    rejudging: Setting\n    61\t    final_verdict: Setting\n    62\t    idea: Setting = None      # the service's reading of a user's idea (docs/poc/idea-card.md); absent in pipeline-only configs\n    63\t    idea_summary: Setting = None  # optional analyst TLDR after the mapping is fixed\n    64\t    collision: Setting = None # D190: the analyst question asked of the generative model when a candidate lists a thesis of its own object and measure; absent: Jev alone\n    65\t    placement: Setting = None # 2026-10-28: the call that files an item's statements under existing theses (extraction v20); absent before v20\n    66\t    baseline: Setting = None  # D364: the coverage-start reading of the company's latest report (hivemind.baseline); absent: no such step\n    67\t\n    68\t    def extraction_setting(self, category):\n    69\t        setting = self.extraction.get(category)\n    70\t        if setting is None:\n    71\t            raise ConfigError(f'no extraction setting for category {category} in config {self.version}')\n    72\t        return setting\n    73\t\n    74\t\n    75\tREQUIRED = ('model', 'prompt_version', 'reasoning_effort', 'max_output_tokens')\n    76\t\n    77\t\n    78\tdef _setting(name, raw):\n    79\t    if not isinstance(raw, dict):\n    80\t        raise ConfigError(f'setting {name} is missing')\n    81\t    missing = [key for key in REQUIRED if key not in raw]\n    82\t    if missing:\n    83\t        raise ConfigError(f'setting {name} lacks {missing}')\n    84\t    engine = str(raw.get('engine', 'llm'))\n    85\t    if engine not in ENGINES:\n    86\t        raise ConfigError(f'setting {name}: engine {engine!r} is not one of {ENGINES}')\n    87\t    return Setting(name=name, model=str(raw['model']), prompt_version=str(raw['prompt_version']),\n    88\t                   reasoning_effort=str(raw['reasoning_effort']),\n    89\t                   max_output_tokens=int(raw['max_output_tokens']), engine=engine)\n    90\t\n    91\t\n    92\tdef load(path):\n    93\t    with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as handle:\n    94\t        text = handle.read()\n    95\t    raw = json.loads(text)\n    96\t    for key in ('version', 'provider', 'search_top_n', 'extraction', 'matching', 'rejudging', 'final_verdict'):\n    97\t        if key not in raw:\n    98\t            raise ConfigError(f'config lacks {key}')\n    99\t    canonical = json.dumps({k: v for k, v in raw.items() if not k.startswith('_')}, sort_keys=True, ensure_ascii=False)\n   100\t    checksum = hashlib.sha256(canonical.encode('utf-8')).hexdigest()[:16]\n   101\t    extraction = {category: _setting(f'extraction.{category}', setting) for category, setting in raw['extraction'].items()}\n   102\t    matching, rejudging = _setting('matching', raw['matching']), _setting('rejudging', raw['rejudging'])\n   103\t    idea = _setting('idea', raw['idea']) if 'idea' in raw else None\n   104\t    idea_summary = _setting('idea_summary', raw['idea_summary']) if 'idea_summary' in raw else None\n   105\t    if idea_summary is not None and idea is None:\n   106\t        raise ConfigError('idea_summary requires an idea setting')\n   107\t    collision = _setting('collision', raw['collision']) if 'collision' in raw else None\n   108\t    placement = _setting('placement', raw['placement']) if 'placement' in raw else None\n   109\t    baseline = _setting('baseline', raw['baseline']) if 'baseline' in raw else None\n   110\t    _prompt_files(extraction, matching, rejudging, idea, idea_summary, collision, placement)       # final_verdict is kept in the file but no POC step calls it\n   111\t    if baseline is not None and not os.path.exists(prompt_path(baseline.prompt_version + '.json')):\n   112\t        raise ConfigError(f'baseline: prompt {baseline.prompt_version}.json does not exist')\n   113\t    return Config(version=str(raw['version']), checksum=checksum, provider=str(raw['provider']),\n   114\t                  search_top_n=int(raw['search_top_n']), extraction=extraction,\n   115\t                  matching=matching, rejudging=rejudging, final_verdict=_setting('final_verdict', raw['final_verdict']), idea=idea, idea_summary=idea_summary, collision=collision, placement=placement,\n   116\t                  baseline=baseline)\nsrc/hivemind/vocabulary.py:8:KINDS = ['asset', 'theme']\nsrc/hivemind/vocabulary.py:18:    'kind': {'对象观点': 'asset', '主题观点': 'theme'},\nsrc/hivemind/extraction.py:86:                    'id': {'type': 'string'}, 'object': {'type': 'string'}, 'kind': {'type': 'string', 'enum': vocabulary.KINDS},\nsrc/hivemind/extraction.py:135:    return candidate.get('kind') == 'theme' or candidate.get('measure') not in FINANCIAL_LINES\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"agent_message","text":"# Council R, round 1 — Linking related questions in poc-v118\n\n**Yes, after named choices and a second $0 review. The design is still short of build-ready.** The matching step should choose which question reads another’s forecast. That link should leave each question’s evidence where it was filed. The judge should receive the other question’s actual forecast, with its assumptions visible.\n\nThe clearest example is old-2. Data Center opened on July 6, and total revenue opened on July 10. On July 12, the system forecast **$400–410B for Data Center and about $387B for all of NVIDIA**. A rule that chooses links only when the reading question opens cannot connect that pair in the useful direction. The September probe inserted the link directly into a copy of the ledger. It showed that the judge can use the link, but skipped how the product would choose and write it. [Stored selection census](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/selection-census.md:59) · [Probe setup](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-probe/probe.py:36)\n\n## 1. Who chooses the link and which way it reads\n\n**Decided.** D51 and D368 item 2 give the choice to matching. A question reads the conclusion it needs. For a segment forecast constrained by the company total, the segment reads the total, as in D537 and the probe. For a forecast that needs a driver’s conclusion, the forecast reads the driver. The reason for each link decides the direction. Council Q applied D368 item 4 too broadly. Its instruction for the root to read the driver concerns the preset arrows described in that item. [D51](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:1265) · [D368](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:3968) · [PRD 06](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/prd/06-views.md:114) · [D537](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:6457)\n\n**Open for the council.** Give Jev a directed choice within matching, using the questions already shown by search and extraction and the questions opened by the same item. Have the model decide whether a conclusion is useful, including an explicit choice of no link. Code verifies the endpoints, exact identities, final opening decisions, labels and absence of cycles. The existing `part_of` answer can inform the model. It says “in either direction,” and in old-1 it scored both FY2027 and FY2028 total revenue as related to FY2027 Data Center. The stored measure, scope, period, basis and unit help the model distinguish those cases. They cannot decide whether reading the conclusion will improve the forecast. The final design still needs an exact request and a refusal path that works with Jev. D51 supplies the existing matching setting and the limit on what may be shown. [Jev criteria and candidate loop](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py:25) · [Selection census](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/selection-census.md:8) · [Ownership table](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/SEPARATION-DESIGN.md:112)\n\n**Decided label meaning, with one scope choice for Alex.** `first_is_bullish` and `last_is_bullish` say which end of the **read question’s** ladder is favourable for the reading asset question. `same` and `opposite` compare the two ladders when the reader is a theme. The label carries no information about which question reads the other or which number is the whole. Validate the choice against the final ladder order, including a successor whose ladder the writer reverses. For an initial version, I recommend using the existing labels only where the model can justify one. If Alex wants links that supply a numerical bound without a defensible favourable end, that needs a separate choice about what the link can say. [Matching instructions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/prompts/matching-v3.txt:10) · [Matching schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/matching.py:30) · [Successor ladder code](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/writing.py:72)\n\nCreation can reach the useful pair in old-1 and old-3. It cannot make the existing Data Center question read the newly opened total in old-2. D191 point 2 already permits a later item to add a link to an existing question. D368 calls for that work to be designed and measured as its own slice. The council still has to specify when that later item may propose the link and how matching records and corrects a conflicting proposal. [Stored selection census](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/selection-census.md:59) · [D191](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:2503) · [D368](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:3968)\n\n## 2. What the judge receives\n\n**Open for the council within a new version Alex opens.** Show the target question, its status, the date and rung of its latest available judgement, its stored `implied_outcome` in full, and a separately bounded excerpt of its reasons. Label the outcome as the system’s forecast. Preserve “waiting” and “not yet judged” when there is no answer to show. The renderer should use the stored outcome directly. `store.current_state` currently omits it, so changing only the line in `judging.py` would still leave it unavailable. The judge already writes the outcome and is already instructed to check a part against its whole. [D260](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:3049) · [Current-state query](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/store.py:230) · [Renderer](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:137) · [Judging-v7](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/prompts/judging-v7.txt:3)\n\nThe three renders identify two different losses. Old-1’s reasons fit within 600 characters but omit Q4. Its stored outcome gives **$112.2B**. Old-3’s reasons reach Q4, but the cut drops the final **$446B** annual forecast. Its stored outcome contains both. Old-2 already showed its decisive figures and improved from 0 of 3 to 2 of 3. The two passing replies still inherited total revenue’s assumption that Q4 grows at Q3’s pace. A complete line makes that assumption visible and testable. [Render check](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-probe/render-check.json) · [Probe result and limits](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-probe/RESULT.md:12)\n\n## 3. Filing\n\n**Alex’s choice.** Scope D265’s automatic removal to a separately established narrower home. A chosen reading link supplies no such hierarchy. D265 currently includes the ledger’s dependency links, and D539–D542 kept the narrower interpretation provisional. With the current rule, adding the link removed seven total-revenue filings. These included the company-wide outlook that the total-revenue judge cited and three statements that each gave both figures. Three segment-related statements would keep their existing double filing under the proposed rule. The correct homes remain a judgement for the evidence-reading step. A narrower-home rule applied to the whole pair cannot distinguish these statements. [D265](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:3084) · [Seven filings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/EDGE-PROOF-RESULT.md:35) · [D540–D541](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:6525) · [Filing code](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/extraction.py:42)\n\n## 4. The five D542 items\n\n| Item | Product contract and status |\n|---|---|\n| **A circle through a waiting question** | **Refusal is decided. The check and retry are open for the council.** Check the complete proposed set before accepting the item, after joining duplicate question identities and resolving waiting years and successors. If A is waiting for B and the item opens B with a link back to A, the check must see both arrows. Today the writer can drop a proposed edge, or leave the waiting edge unbound silently, depending on where the check fires. PRD 06 calls for correction and normal failure if the cycle persists. Jev currently gets one pass because sending the same request again gives the same answer. The new design must specify what correction is sent and what gets recorded if it fails. [PRD 06](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/prd/06-views.md:133) · [Writer](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/writing.py:175) · [Waiting resolution](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/writing.py:49) · [Matching retry](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/round.py:35) |\n| **Closing** | **Behaviour is decided. Where to implement it is open for the council.** A closed number question keeps supplying its frozen answer for the specified period. A closed event supplies background marked “historical only” until a successor exists. I recommend deriving that mark from the recorded closing and the successor visible at the read boundary. Record a move to the successor with the item that creates it, after the cycle check, and queue the reader once that day. Closing alone starts no judge call. The current renderer recognizes `history_only`, but the production resolution writer only records waiting-year bindings. [PRD 06](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/prd/06-views.md:118) · [Renderer](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:137) · [Pull close](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/pull.py:30) |\n| **A theme shown twice** | **Open for the council.** Keep D195’s theme coverage. When an explicitly linked theme is already in the asset question’s theme block, show its conclusion once there, include the link’s direction there, and use `theme_pushes` for that ID. A theme reader has no theme block, so a link from one theme to another uses the dependency line. Build the citation checks from what the request actually showed. Check the schedule too. A link that requires an asset to be judged before a theme it is meant to see creates a conflict with same-round theme delivery. [D195](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:2538) · [Theme rendering and citation checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:137) · [Ready order](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:306) |\n| **Historical readers** | **Date semantics are decided. Reader changes and proof are open for the council.** Service, search, bundle and the idea route use the requested day. Research context uses its exclusive `before_seq` boundary. At that boundary, pick the latest visible target and use it for both directions. Keep the dated history so the UI can show the earlier target. Today the service’s reverse lookup counts old targets as well as the latest one, and the bundle’s reverse lookup omits resolutions entirely. D542’s check of 399 questions at one late date did not exercise either transition. Prove these readers before a binding, on its day, and after a move. [Service](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/service.py:178) · [Bundle](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/bundle.py:150) · [Search](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/search.py:284) · [Research context](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/research_context.py:34) · [Idea route](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/idea.py:144) · [UI date handling](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/ui/src/model/thesis.js:80) |\n| **`last_end`** | **Decided for this scope.** It has no role in the product version. The three finished poc-v118 stores have zero dependency rows. The product’s matching labels are the four in item 1. Preserve the supported reading of identifiable historical matching-era links without rewriting their history. [D541–D542](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:6544) · [Label vocabulary](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/vocabulary.py:13) |\n\n## 5. Daily order, propagation and cost\n\n**Decided.** If both questions are due to be judged, judge the one being read first. If only the reader is due, it gets the latest available conclusion. If the question being read reports a changed conclusion, queue its eligible reader once in the same round. A change to a key figure counts inside the same rung. A resumed round recovers the queue from judgements already committed at its sequence number. Closed questions and questions without their own evidence are ineligible. Ordering and propagation must use the same current target, including after a successor takes over. Today `topological` can fall back to input order on a cycle, which makes the write-time check essential. [D51](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:1265) · [Order](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:102) · [Queue and resume](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:266)\n\nFor an isolated pair, the increase is **zero or one accepted judge call per day**. It is zero if both were already queued, the upstream conclusion did not change, or the reader is ineligible. It is one if the link is the sole reason an eligible reader is queued. Initial binding or moving to a successor can also queue that reader once. Retries and any wider chain are separate. At the published Luna rates of **$0.10/M input, $0.01/M cached input and $0.50/M output**, one extra call the size of old-2’s September 20 Data Center call would cost an estimated **$0.0056** before cache discounts. Existing calls that receive the line also incur its added input tokens. [Rates and usage calculation](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/model.py:26)\n\n## 6. What must be complete before build\n\nThe four AGENTS §2 tables can be completed for this product. The following are the remaining entries that prevent signing them off.\n\n| Required table | What still needs to be pinned down |\n|---|---|\n| **State × event** | Specify the atomic transition for a proposed, waiting, bound, closed or successor-following link at the item’s owned sequence. Specify success, refusal, correction, persistent refusal, and the crash before or after the write marker. A repeat of the same pair and label writes nothing. A different label on the same pair needs a recorded conflict outcome. Reuse the item’s sequence on resume, validate against the state that will be committed, and use the existing single-writer boundary for the store. Prove the route with a changed input or configuration and a second writer attempt. The parallel manifest already detects changed runtime, configuration and inputs. [Atomic write](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/writing.py:108) · [Resume](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/dispatch.py:23) · [Parallel manifest](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/parallel_day.py:68) |\n| **Value × reader** | Pin the matching answer shape, the writer’s link and resolution values, and the live and historical readers in item 4. Include the matching record, the filing snapshot, judgement ordering and citations, and the saved judgement and its consumers. Specify how old matching labels and missing old forecasts are read. New links must have an explicit writer-owned meaning. [Existing answer shape](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/matching.py:30) · [Existing store shape](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/store.py:13) |\n| **New path × old checks** | Exercise exact-identity coalescing, opens and successors, shown IDs, valid labels, direct versus waiting inputs, duplicates and conflicts, and cycles through the committed graph. Replay the seven affected filings and an unrelated pair. Check the new request’s exact citation set and same-day order, plus the historical readers across a resolution. The archived v118 requests contain no chosen links, so preflight alone leaves these paths unexercised. The earlier whole-week proof also supplied the old judge answers after changing their requests. [Matching checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/matching.py:268) · [Preflight coverage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/scripts/preflight.py:226) · [Whole-week proof limits](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/SEPARATION-DESIGN.md:285) |\n| **Prior decisions** | Record the D265 interpretation explicitly. Apply D51 to selection, D191 and D368 item 2 to the later addition, D195 to themes, D356 to closing, and D354 to versioning. D542 requires the above gaps to be settled before build. D692 sends this review to Alex before any build or send. D439 gives a recorded council consensus authority during Alex’s absence. This independent round supplies no such consensus. [D542](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:6566) · [D692](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:10398) · [D439](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4871) |\n\n**The freeze applies to the requests the product builds.** A new directed Jev comparison changes matching. A richer dependency line or different citation instructions changes the judge request even if the `judging-v7.txt` bytes and answer schema stay the same. Open a new explicit configuration and preserve the pinned control. The no-dependency request is the byte-equality control. The clean Step 2 freeze covers a different path. [D354](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:3685) · [D359](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:3798) · [Frozen product checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/tests/test_frozen_questions_and_judgement.py:14) · [Clean Step 2 checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/tests/test_frozen_clean_step2.py:9)\n\n## Alex’s choices and the one test\n\n1. **What a reading link does to evidence.** Choose between keeping the existing automatic removal and taking reading links out of that rule. I recommend taking them out. The total-revenue question should keep its own outlook when Data Center starts reading its forecast.\n2. **Which capability to open.** Choose between a creation-only first release and opening the already-decided later-addition work as a second, separately reviewed slice. I recommend the second if the goal includes the pair that motivated this review. Use the current direction labels for this first version. A link that only sets a bound and has no meaningful favourable end merits a separate scope decision if the council finds a case the existing vocabulary cannot express.\n3. **Whether to authorize the version and its bounded test after the $0 review.** I recommend authorizing the new matching and judging request versions once the checks above are exercised, then running the comparison below. Keep poc-v118 as the control.\n\n**One test.** Use old-2 from just before the July 10 item at sequence 148 through July 12. First rebuild the stored control at $0 and prove that the new path can select and write **existing Data Center reads newly opened total revenue** through real matching and writing. Exercise the refusal, filing, historical-read and resume cases with stored answers or labelled fakes. For the paid comparison, run three fresh control sequences and three linked sequences from the same starting state. Buy only the affected calls. Replay unrelated calls by exact request identity and stop on an unexpected miss. Each sequence must use its own accepted forecasts and citations on the next day. A failed selection counts as a failed sequence. [Stored selection case](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/selection-census.md:59) · [Why the earlier fixed-answer replay cannot substitute](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/SEPARATION-DESIGN.md:285)\n\nHave independent readers compare both forecasts with the sources available on each day. Check the same fiscal period and units, the reported figures, the arithmetic, and every assumption that materially sizes the answer. The part must fit inside the whole without making either forecast less defensible. A range with no comparable figure leaves that comparison untested. A control that usually agrees makes the benefit inconclusive. Report all six sequences, including whether the judge actually used the linked forecast.\n\n**Estimated cost is $0.06–$0.07 before retries.** The five stored pair judgements in that window used 31,158 input and 13,293 output tokens. Six sequences at those uncached sizes cost about $0.059. Allow roughly $0.001 for three Jev choices at the stored opening request’s size and up to about $0.009 if propagation adds a Data Center call on July 11 in all three linked sequences. The estimate assumes unrelated requests replay and the new choice stays near the stored input size. Set the cap from the rebuilt requests before any send. [Published rates used by the product](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/model.py:26)\n\nThis was a read-only review. I checked the three finished ledgers and the stored answers. The proposed selection, lifecycle and reader contracts still require independent review and the $0 proof."}}
